ent        0 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_citation_weave_agent_codex_20261117_131854.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    81670 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_citation_weave_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     4256 Nov 17 14:17 postdraft_reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     4553 Nov 17 14:17 postdraft_reference_query_plan.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent   239229 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_131854.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     2559 Nov 17 14:14 postdraft_reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      279 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      451 Nov 17 14:18 postdraft_reference_retrieval.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7549 Nov 17 14:00 postdraft_references.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    20904 Nov 17 14:00 postdraft_references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     9086 Nov 17 13:23 reference_query_plan.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     9751 Nov 17 13:23 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent  1731611 Nov 17 13:24 reference_query_plan_agent_codex_20261117_131854.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    32726 Nov 17 13:19 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov 17 13:24 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      279 Nov 17 13:24 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     4284 Nov 17 13:28 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7549 Nov 17 14:18 references.bib\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     1427 Nov 17 14:10 references.bib.prov.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     1041 Nov 17 14:18 references.openalex.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     1386 Nov 17 14:18 references.openalex.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent     7549 Nov 17 14:18 references.pre_postdraft.bib\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    20904 Nov 17 14:18 references.pre_postdraft.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent    21342 Nov 17 14:18 references_provenance.json\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent     1439 Nov 17 14:10 references_provenance.json.prov.json\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent       16 Nov 17 13:18 results -> ../investigation\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 agent agent     4096 Nov 17 14:10 scientific-evidence\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 agent agent     4096 Nov 17 14:08 scripts\ndrwx------  5 agent agent     4096 Nov 17 13:57 source_evidence\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      265 Nov 17 13:39 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw-------  1 agent agent      271 Nov 17 13:39 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx  1 agent agent        7 Nov 17 13:18 task -> ../task\n-rw-r--r--  1 agent agent      457 Nov 17 13:55 template_provenance.json\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../investigation/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/vercel/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/supabase-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/build-web-apps/skills/react-best-practices/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/data-analytics/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/zoom/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/prototype/AGENTS.md\n../task_drafting/_task_drafting/draft_20261117_111439_1794914079641849011/.agent_home/.codex/.tmp/plugins/plugins/product-design/templates/mobile-app/AGENTS.md\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\ncat: /workspace/paper/.codex/skills/{memory-log,scientific-evidence,write-paper-standalone,validate-paper,generate-concept-figure}/SKILL.md: No such file or directory\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"for name in memory-log scientific-evidence write-paper-standalone validate-paper generate-concept-figure; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$name\"; cat .codex/skills/$name/SKILL.md; done'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"for name in memory-log scientific-evidence write-paper-standalone validate-paper generate-concept-figure; do printf '\\\\n--- %s ---\\\\n' \\\"\"'$name\"; cat .codex/skills/$name/SKILL.md; done'","aggregated_output":"\n--- memory-log ---\n---\nname: memory-log\ndescription: Maintain a continuous, append-only `./memory.md` log of discoveries, evaluations, failures, and next hypotheses during agent runs.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nKeep durable run memory so idea evolution and program evidence are never lost between steps.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- At run start to initialize memory.\n- After every meaningful step (`idea`, `edit`, `evaluate`, `verify`, `paper`, `draft`).\n- Immediately after every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair.\n\n# What to do\n1. Initialize `./memory.md` if missing with these section headers:\n- `# Memory Log`\n- `## Context`\n- `## Idea Discoveries`\n- `## Program Evaluations`\n- `## Failures / Regressions`\n- `## Next Hypotheses`\n2. Also initialize global files if missing:\n- `./global_memory/memory.md`\n- `./global_memory/memory.jsonl`\n3. Never delete or rewrite prior entries; append only.\n4. For each appended entry, include:\n- timestamp\n- step type\n- change/discovery summary\n- concrete evidence\n- next action\n5. After every `evaluate-program -> verify-rubrics` pair, append a `Program Evaluations` entry that includes:\n- `program_<idx>`\n- `combined_score`\n- `correct`\n- rubric pass/fail summary\n- `next_recommendation`\n6. For regressions or failed rubrics, append one root-cause hypothesis under `Failures / Regressions`.\n7. Before exit, append final status (`rubric_pass`, `cap_reached`, or task-specific stop reason) and next hypothesis under `Next Hypotheses`.\n8. When command execution is unavailable, use read/edit/write tools to append directly\nto the required markdown and JSONL files. Preserve all existing content. When command\nexecution is available, prefer the helper for deterministic dual-write:\n- script: detect the active agent config directory first, then use `<that-dir>/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py`\n- command template:\n```bash\nMEMORY_HELPER=\"$(find ./.claude ./.codex ./.cursor ./.opencode ./.gemini ./.pi -path '*/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py' -print -quit 2>/dev/null)\"\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" \\\n  --global-memory-md \"./global_memory/memory.md\" \\\n  --global-memory-jsonl \"./global_memory/memory.jsonl\" \\\n  --event-json '{\"step\":\"evaluate\",\"scope\":\"attempt\",\"summary\":\"...\",\"evidence\":\"...\",\"next_action\":\"...\",\"attempt_id\":\"{{ATTEMPT_ID}}\",\"idea_id\":\"{{IDEA_ID}}\",\"program_id\":\"program_2\",\"metrics\":{\"combined_score\":2.54,\"correct\":true}}'\n```\n9. JSONL event schema (required keys):\n- `timestamp` (auto-filled if omitted)\n- `step` (`idea|edit|evaluate|verify|paper|draft|final|conclusions_skip`)\n- `scope` (`idea|attempt|paper|campaign`)\n- `summary`\n- `evidence`\n- `next_action`\n- optional: `idea_id`, `attempt_id`, `program_id`, `metrics`, `rubrics`, `tags`\n\n10. Update distilled conclusions (`conclusions.md`).\n**Purpose.** `conclusions.md` is a *forward-looking guide* for the next worker (or your future self in a later cycle). Its job is to help readers (a) focus their effort on directions you've found promising, and (b) avoid spending compute on directions you've already shown to be unpromising. Each sentence should either point at a lead worth pursuing or warn against a dead end, framed so a researcher who has not read your `memory.md` can still act on it. Avoid chronology, restate-of-results, and post-mortem narration — those belong in `memory.md`. Conclusions are the synthesis layer that makes other workers' next cycles more efficient.\n\n**When.** After every memory-append in the cycle (idea / edit / evaluate / verify / paper / final), evaluate whether `conclusions.md` should be updated.\n\n**Update gate (the only valid triggers).**\n- (a) cycle **contradicts** an existing sentence → rewrite that sentence.\n- (b) cycle opens a **new category** not yet covered → add a sentence.\n- (c) cycle **flips a Do/Don't** recommendation → flip it.\n- (d) cycle **meaningfully refines** an existing sentence — narrows the conditions under which a rule holds, broadens its applicability to a config it wasn't known to apply to, sharpens a tentative claim into a confident one, or adds a qualifier the prior phrasing didn't cover → edit the sentence to incorporate the refinement. Simple re-confirmation in the same regime is **not** sufficient; you must identify the refinement dimension (narrow / broaden / sharpen / qualify) in `--gate-reason`.\n- otherwise → **keep**, do not invoke the helper.\n\n**Format constraints.** Single paragraph. ≤300 words. No blank lines (`\\n\\n`). Avoid detailed numerical figures — use qualitative thresholds (e.g. \"low LR\", \"small batch\") instead of precise floats like `LR=0.205`.\n\n**Update mechanism.** When execution is unavailable, apply the same validation rules\nand update the conclusions files directly with read/edit/write tools. When execution\nis available, use this helper invocation:\n```bash\npython \"${MEMORY_HELPER}\" --write-conclusions \\\n  --conclusions-md \"{{CONCLUSIONS_MD_FILE}}\" \\\n  --conclusions-history-jsonl \"{{CONCLUSIONS_HISTORY_FILE}}\" \\\n  --cycle-index {{CYCLE_INDEX}} \\\n  --worker-index {{WORKER_INDEX}} \\\n  --gate-trigger <a|b|c|d> \\\n  --gate-reason \"<one sentence; for (d) name the dimension>\" \\\n  --conclusions-text \"<the full new paragraph>\"\n```\n\n**Pre-flight self-check.** Before invoking the helper, count words (`len(paragraph.split())`) and confirm ≤300, and that the paragraph contains no blank line. Most validation rejections can be avoided here.\n\n**Retry budget — exactly 3 attempts per cycle.** On non-zero exit, read the stderr error, shorten the paragraph (drop the least-decisive sentence, merge two related sentences, or remove qualifiers), and retry. After **3 total attempts** with no success, the update is **discarded** for this cycle: do not retry further, do not write the rejected text anywhere, and append a single memory event with `step=\"conclusions_skip\"` whose `summary` names the last validation error. Underlying findings are not lost — they are already durable in `memory.md`.\n\n**Always log the gate decision.** Inside the regular memory append for the cycle, include `gate=<updated|kept|skipped>; trigger=<a|b|c|d|none>; reason=<one sentence>` in the entry's `summary` or `tags`. Use `skipped` only after the 3-attempt budget is exhausted.\n\n# Example evaluation entry\n```markdown\n### 2026-02-13T16:58:00Z | step=evaluate\n- program: program_3\n- combined_score: 2.54\n- correct: true\n- rubrics: uses_restarts=pass, constraint_optimization=pass, score_threshold=fail\n- evidence: late-stage overlap still visible in stdout trace\n- next_recommendation: raise late-stage overlap penalty and diversify restart seeds\n```\n\n--- scientific-evidence ---\n---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n\n--- write-paper-standalone ---\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n\n--- validate-paper ---\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n\n--- generate-concept-figure ---\n---\nname: generate-concept-figure\ndescription: Generate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for paper generation as an editable draw.io diagram via the pinned Autoillustrate npm release, with a deterministic layout and an optional image-model restyle.\n---\n\n# Who runs this (important)\nDuring **paper generation the orchestrator owns this pipeline**: `src/scripts/paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` run the installed `autoillustrate` CLI synchronously as a post-draft step (`run_concept_figure_step`), derive every argument from `paper_strategy.json`, forward the selected outer agent, and promote the result to `./figures/<figure_id>.png`. The phase agents always run in a dedicated one-shot Docker container, even when the outer paper workflow is native; this limits them to the paper directory, selected-agent auth, and selected provider credentials. The paper-writing agent must **not** invoke this pipeline itself. The rest of this document describes the same CLI for standalone/manual use.\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate one reader-facing conceptual overview figure for the paper when a high-level glance-value visual will clarify the method, comparison story, or evidence structure. The figure is produced by the Autoillustrate pipeline (the `autoillustrate` npm release pinned in the agent image): each panel is a collision-checked, deterministically laid-out draw.io diagram, the panels are composed into a single editable `.drawio` file, and the composite is optionally restyled into a camera-ready PNG. The deliverable is a real `.drawio` source plus its export — only the final restyle pass touches a generative model.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper generation.\n- The paper needs an early conceptual figure that is easier to scan than a dense quantitative plot.\n- The grounded artifacts support a clean overview figure without inventing unsupported mechanisms or claims.\n- `paper_strategy.json` requests a required overview-style `diagram` figure or the paper prompt calls for an overview figure on page 1 or 2.\n- The figure is a methods/architecture/training-loop schematic with 1–3 structurally distinct panels. (For pure quantitative plots use matplotlib/seaborn instead.)\n\n# Prerequisites\nThe pipeline shells out to several tools. Confirm them before claiming a hard failure:\n- **`drawio` desktop CLI** on `PATH` (headless export). Without it Phases 2/4 cannot export.\n- **`autoillustrate` and `headless`** on `PATH`, plus the selected agent CLI (`claude`, `codex`, `cursor`, `gemini`, `opencode`, or `pi`).\n- For environment-authenticated OpenCode phase agents, set `PAPER_CONCEPT_FIGURE_AGENT_PROVIDER` to exactly one of `openai`, `gemini`, or `anthropic`; only that provider credential is forwarded.\n- **`OPENAI_API_KEY`** — the default VLM backend (`gpt-5.5`) for the Phase 3 per-panel inspection and the Phase 6 verification, and the image model (`gpt-image-2`) for the Phase 5 restyle. Without it those VLM calls and the restyle cannot run; you can still ship the deterministic Phase 4 export with `--skip-enhance`.\n- **`GEMINI_API_KEY`** — optional fallback. When OpenAI is unavailable, the paper orchestrator selects `nanobanana-pro`, `gemini-3.1-pro`, and `gemini-single` for enhancement and audits.\n- **A Python venv** with the toolkit deps. The container image bakes one and exposes its interpreter as `$VENV_PY`.\n\nBefore deciding a key is missing, check the active environment directly. OpenAI is the default; Gemini is the automatic fallback:\n```bash\npython - <<'PY'\nimport os\nfor k in (\"OPENAI_API_KEY\", \"GEMINI_API_KEY\"):\n    print(f\"{k}: {'available' if os.getenv(k) else 'missing'}\")\nPY\n```\nDo not claim a key is unavailable when the rendered paper prompt says it is expected to be available unless your direct check still shows it missing. Without an enhancement key, `--skip-enhance` still ships the deterministic Phase 4 export.\n\n# What to do\n1. Infer the central mechanism, comparison, or evidence story from grounded artifacts only. Decide whether one cohesive panel suffices or the content naturally splits into 2–3 structurally independent panels (e.g. *architecture · update rule · training loop*). Prefer one overview figure, not a full illustration suite.\n\n2. Confirm the installed CLI and selected agent are available:\n```bash\ncommand -v autoillustrate\nautoillustrate doctor --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\"\n```\n\n3. In the project container, use the baked `$VENV_PY`. Native installations may let Autoillustrate create its cached venv or set `VENV_PY` explicitly.\n\n4. Run the pipeline against the manuscript. Resolve the strategy's exact figure id, pass `--output ./figures/<figure_id>.png`, forward the selected `--agent`, and provide a concise grounded prompt. Add `--skip-enhance` when no enhancement backend is available.\n\n**This pipeline can take ~1h** (it dispatches several selected-agent jobs and may run a VLM restyle). Run it **synchronously and block on it** — the caller must own the pipeline's full lifetime. Do **not** launch it as a background task that outlives the caller's turn (`run_in_background: true`, a trailing `&`, or a fire-and-forget agent step): when that turn ends the pipeline is orphaned and reaped mid-run, leaving the figure unrendered. In `paper.sh` and `paper_edit.sh` this is exactly what `run_concept_figure_step` does — a single synchronous invocation in the agent container, wrapped with `timeout` or Homebrew's `gtimeout` so a genuine hang is bounded. Native systems without either command skip this non-gating render step instead of launching an unbounded process. The pipeline writes `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (and `<figure_id>.prompts.json`) only on success; confirm those files exist before treating the figure as done.\n```bash\nautoillustrate ./paper.tex \\\n  --output ./figures/<figure_id>.png \\\n  --agent \"${AGENT:-claude}\" \\\n  --prompt \"Methods diagram that will be the main figure of this scientific research paper. <one or two sentences grounding the panels in the actual method>\"\n```\nThe orchestrator creates `runs/<YYYY-MM-DD-HHMMSS>/` in the working directory and writes the deliverables to `runs/<ts>/figures/`:\n- `full_figure.drawio` — editable draw.io source\n- `full_figure.png` — deterministic export (no model involved; always safe)\n- `full_figure_enhanced.png` — publication-grade restyle (only when Phase 5 ran)\n- `figure.json` — the validated 1–3 panel plan that drove the figure\n\nWith `--output` set, the pipeline also copies the best available figure (the enhanced export when Phase 5 ran, otherwise the deterministic export) to `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and drops the plan beside it as `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`. The pipeline does not emit a PDF; the editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir.\n\n5. Keep the visual style scientific and simple: clean diagrammatic composition, exact labels, no decorative clip-art, no unsupported architecture details, datasets, or numeric claims. When the paper contains multiple figures, keep colors logically consistent — reuse the same color for the same method/baseline/condition across figures.\n\n6. Confirm the promoted deliverable. With `--output` (step 4) the pipeline already wrote `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` — verify both exist. If you ran without `--output`, promote manually from the run workdir (enhanced export preferred, deterministic export as fallback):\n```bash\nRUN_FIGS=\"$(ls -td runs/*/figures | head -1)\"\nSRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure_enhanced.png\"; [[ -f \"$SRC_PNG\" ]] || SRC_PNG=\"$RUN_FIGS/full_figure.png\"\ncp \"$SRC_PNG\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.png\"\ncp \"$RUN_FIGS/figure.json\" \"./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json\"   # the prompt/plan log\n```\nThe editable `full_figure.drawio` stays in the run workdir so the figure can be re-edited later. The pipeline produces no PDF; the manifest does not require one for this figure.\n\n7. When the strategy or prompt asks for a required overview/`diagram` figure, do not silently replace this workflow with a hand-drawn or purely matplotlib/TikZ schematic. Use this skill first unless it is impossible.\n\n8. If a prerequisite is missing (for example, no `drawio` CLI) or the pipeline fails, log an explicit skip/failure reason in both `./memory.md` and `./global_memory/memory.md` before falling back to another figure implementation. Apply the same fallback logging rule if the generated figure is too poor to use.\n\n9. If you fall back to a non-drawio overview figure, record that fallback reason in the matching `figure_manifest.json` note so the run leaves a clear breadcrumb.\n\n10. If the final figure is used in `paper.tex`, add a matching `figure_manifest.json` entry that records `generator: generate-concept-figure`, `prompt_log_path` (the copied `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json`), and any `fallback_reason`. If the figure is not used, do not force it into the manuscript.\n\n# Outputs\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.png` (promoted from `full_figure_enhanced.png`, or `full_figure.png` when enhancement was skipped)\n- `./figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` (the run's `figure.json` plan, used as `prompt_log_path`)\n- Editable source preserved at `runs/<ts>/figures/full_figure.drawio`\n- Legacy explicit-path form remains valid: `./figures/concept_figure.png` and `./figures/concept_figure.prompts.json`\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc 'cat .codex/skills/scientific-evidence/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: scientific-evidence\ndescription: |\n  Use when: you start writing a scientific paper.\n  Outputs: a repo-local `scientific-evidence/` bundle generated by the skill script, plus the conventions, schemas, and verification report needed for downstream evidence tracking and auditing.\n---\n\n# scientific-evidence\n\nUse this skill to define or materialize a generic evidence contract for scientific artifacts.\n\nAlways use this skill during paper writing whenever the agent adds quantitative content: manuscript numbers, table entries, figure inputs, derived statistics, or plotting/calculation scripts.\n\n## What this skill is for\n\nThe point of this skill is simple: an agent should not be able to drop important numbers, figures, or derived outputs into a paper without leaving a machine-checkable trail back to real artifacts.\n\nThis skill gives the agent one fixed place and one fixed grammar for that trail:\n- inline `@evidence[[...]]` refs in text-like files\n- `.prov.json` sidecars for generated artifacts\n- a central `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl` for optional structured rollups\n\nIt is deliberately generic. The base contract works even when the workflow spans Python, bash, LaTeX, Markdown, JSON, logs, or other file formats.\n\n## Default workflow\n\n1. Read the current paper/results layout, but do not invent a new evidence layout.\n2. Use the fixed directory structure rooted at `scientific-evidence/`.\n3. Run the skill script first instead of inventing schemas by hand:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n4. For every quantitative paper-writing action, use this skill contract:\n- adding a number to `paper.tex`\n- filling a table cell\n- writing a plotting script\n- writing a calculation/statistics script\n- generating a figure, table, or derived JSON file\n5. Inside plotting or calculation scripts, do not hardcode experiment numbers that should come from artifacts. Read them from files, then leave the corresponding `@evidence[[...]]` refs near the read/derivation logic.\n6. Add inline evidence refs in text-like artifacts and `.prov.json` sidecars for generated outputs.\n7. Run the verifier after the agent writes claims, scripts, figures, or sidecars:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir <workspace-root>\n```\n8. Inspect `scientific-evidence/verification-report.json` and fail hard on dangling links.\n9. Treat the generated markdown spec and JSON Schemas as canonical for downstream agents.\nKeep the verifier behavior explicit:\n- `resolved`: target artifact exists and the verifier can check the locator semantics it understands.\n- `anchored`: target artifact exists, but the locator stays opaque or only hash/path checks were possible.\n- `dangling`: the target or manifest link does not resolve.\n\n## How this is used to verify agent work\n\nVerification works by checking whether the agent left a connected evidence trail.\n\nAt a minimum, a downstream verifier should be able to:\n- scan manuscript text, scripts, and notes for `@evidence[[...]]` refs\n- read `.prov.json` sidecars for figures, tables, and other generated outputs\n- resolve those refs against real files or stable ids\n- classify each link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`\n- fail when a generated artifact has no input trail or when a claimed source does not exist\n\nThis does not prove every scientific claim automatically. It does prove whether the agent left enough structure for an auditor or stricter verifier to replay and inspect the work instead of trusting free-form prose.\n\nA core policy for paper writing is: do not hardcode result numbers inside plotting or calculation scripts when those values should be read from real artifacts. Read from files, derive from those inputs, and attach `@evidence[[...]]` refs that point back to the inputs.\n\n## Fixed directory structure\n\n```text\nscientific-evidence/\n  spec.md\n  bundle.manifest.json\n  ledger.jsonl\n  schemas/\n    evidence-reference.schema.json\n    artifact-manifest.schema.json\n    ledger-entry.schema.json\n    verification-report.schema.json\n```\n\n## Grammar summary\n\n- Evidence ref token: `@evidence[[TARGET]]` or `@evidence[[TARGET#LOCATOR]]`\n- `TARGET` may be a relative path, absolute path, URI, or stable identifier.\n- `LOCATOR` is opaque unless a verifier adapter knows how to interpret it.\n- Generated artifact sidecar name: `<artifact>.prov.json`\n- Central ledger file: `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Schema set\n\nGenerate the canonical schema bundle with the script. The bundle includes:\n- `scientific-evidence/spec.md`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/evidence-reference.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/artifact-manifest.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/ledger-entry.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/schemas/verification-report.schema.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/bundle.manifest.json`\n- `scientific-evidence/ledger.jsonl`\n\n## Examples\n\nPython:\n```python\n# Read the metric from disk; do not hardcode 96.7 here.\n# @evidence[[results/metrics.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nimport json\npayload = json.loads(open(\"results/metrics.json\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())\nvalue = payload[\"mnist\"][\"accuracy_mean\"]\n```\n\nBash:\n```bash\n# @evidence[[results/run_summary.csv#row(dataset=mnist,variant=proposed).accuracy]]\npython scripts/summarize_results.py --input results/run_summary.csv\n```\n\nLaTeX:\n```tex\nAccuracy reached 96.7\\%. % @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\n```\n\nMarkdown:\n```md\nMain result: 96.7%. <!-- @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]] -->\n```\n\nR:\n```r\n# @evidence[[results/summary.tsv#humanoid.edppo.mean]]\nsummary <- read.delim(\"results/summary.tsv\")\nvalue <- subset(summary, method == \"ED-PPO\" & env == \"Humanoid\")$mean[[1]]\n```\n\nYAML:\n```yaml\n# @evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\nreported_metric: mnist_accuracy_mean\n```\n\nJSON sidecar:\n```json\n{\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.artifact-manifest\",\n  \"artifact\": {\"path\": \"figures/main.png\", \"kind\": \"figure\"},\n  \"derived_from\": [\n    \"@evidence[[generated/stats.json#mnist.accuracy_mean]]\",\n    \"@evidence[[scripts/build_figure.py]]\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n## Verification\n\nSmoke test the script, bundle generation, and verifier:\n```bash\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh --help\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\nbash src/agents/skills/scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh verify --root-dir /tmp/scientific-evidence-example\n```\n---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n---\nname: write-paper-standalone\ndescription: Write a grounded LaTeX paper as one coherent standalone scientific study centered on a single defensible thesis rather than the run chronology.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nGenerate `paper.tex` and `claims_grounding.json` from grounded artifacts only, with a clean standalone-paper narrative.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- Final paper step when the default paper-writing skill keeps drifting into a search diary.\n- Need a standalone scientific paper built around one central method, hypothesis, or comparison story.\n- Need to hide run order and expose only the subset of baselines and ablations that support a coherent grounded manuscript.\n\n# What to do\n1. Read:\n- `results_readme.md` — campaign-level overview of all tasks, variants, and where to find results. Use this to understand the full scope of the campaign beyond the primary task passed via `--task-dir`.\n- `paper_data.json`\n- `references.bib`\n- `references_provenance.json`\n- `contributions.md` (if provided) — optional base for framing the claimed contributions; not a grounding source\n- `global_memory_summary.md` (if provided)\n- Aggregated CSVs in `/` for fine-grained analysis (install and use pandas) — when available\n- generated figures in `figures/`\n- when `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also follow `presentation_contract` as a visible-language contract\n2. Use `templates/iclr/paper.tex` to keep section order stable. The paper format is **iclr**.\nInspect the selected template and any venue guidance before drafting so the author block, section structure, and framing expectations match the chosen format.\n3. Before drafting, choose one central thesis that is strongly supported by grounded evidence. Do not choose the paper story by recency alone.\n4. Group related implementations into a small number of named method families. Keep only the baseline, proposed method, and ablations needed to support the central thesis.\n5. If the artifacts do not support one coherent paper story, fail clearly instead of narrating the full search process.\n6. Write required sections:\n- abstract\n- introduction\n- related work\n- method\n- results\n- conclusion\n- references\nThe Supplementary Information section is mandatory.\nEmbed it in the same `paper.tex`, after the references, in this order: `\\clearpage`, bibliography, `\\clearpage`, `\\appendix`, and `\\section{Supplementary Information}`.\nReplace the template scaffold with substantive grounded content, including at least one meaningful subsection, table, or figure supported by the available artifacts. Suitable material includes extended methods, reproducibility details, additional ablations, robustness or failure analyses, and extra grounded tables or figures. Never move evidence essential to the central claim out of the main paper.\nRoute `appendix`-ranked posthoc analyses to Supplementary Information by default. Do not promote or drop them merely because of the main-body page budget.\n7. Generate comparative figures with Python + matplotlib by constructing analysis code yourself during the run:\n- inline `python - <<'PY' ... PY`, or\n- ad-hoc scripts in the paper workspace (for reproducibility).\n8. Prefer comparison figures over chronology figures. Use progression-style plots only when they directly support the main scientific claim.\n9. When multiple benchmarks, datasets, or settings are included, organize the visible paper around a shared hypothesis and condition-specific adaptations or limits, not around campaign chronology.\n10. If `paper_strategy.json` includes `figure_specs`, implement those figures first and keep every generated figure within the declared readability budgets.\n11. Use a logically consistent color scheme across all figures in the paper. Reuse the same colors for the same methods, baselines, conditions, or semantic roles whenever they recur, and avoid remapping meanings between figures without a clear reason.\n12. When a `figure_specs[*]` entry sets `preferred_generator` to `generate-concept-figure`, your job is to wire it in, not to render it. Do not invoke `autoillustrate` or the `generate-concept-figure` pipeline. Reference `./figures/<figure_id>.png` in the manuscript, create a lightweight placeholder PNG and `{}` prompt-log stub so the draft compiles, and register `generator: generate-concept-figure` plus `prompt_log_path: figures/<figure_id>.prompts.json` in `figure_manifest.json`. The orchestrator replaces those placeholders synchronously after the draft stage. Any reusable placeholder script must skip generation when `.concept_figure_complete` exists so repair rounds cannot overwrite the rendered asset.\n13. Do not satisfy a required overview-style `diagram` figure with a hand-built matplotlib/TikZ schematic unless the concept-figure workflow failed or was impossible; if that fallback happens, record the reason in `./memory.md`, `./global_memory/memory.md`, and the matching `figure_manifest.json` entry.\n14. Write `figure_manifest.json` for every figure referenced in `paper.tex`. Use `../validate-paper/templates/figure_manifest.schema.json` as the canonical contract. Legacy flat rows and richer nested rows are both acceptable if they validate against that schema.\n15. Record generator provenance in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `generator`, `prompt_log_path`, and `fallback_reason` when a preferred generator was skipped.\nWhen evidence-audit feedback requires changing an orchestrator-owned concept figure, turn the correction into a complete documented fallback: export both `./figures/<figure_id>.png` and `./figures/<figure_id>.pdf`, set `generator` to the actual fallback generator (`matplotlib`, `tikz`, `native_latex`, or `manual`), set `attempted_generator: generate-concept-figure`, retain the original `prompt_log_path`, and set a non-empty `fallback_reason` describing why the orchestrator render was replaced. Use exactly those canonical artifact paths, and do not create or modify `./figures/<figure_id>.png.prov.json`; the orchestrator issues request-bound provenance after accepting the repair. Do not patch only the PNG while leaving `generator: generate-concept-figure`; that falsely claims ownership and allows figure preparation to restore the old render. Do not alter `paper_strategy.json` solely to bypass concept-figure orchestration, and do not create, delete, or modify `.concept_figure_complete`; the orchestrator owns that marker.\n16. Before choosing figure widths, inspect the selected paper template or style files to understand the practical column width, full-width span, and text footprint for the current paper format.\n17. Record final-layout hints in `figure_manifest.json` when possible: `placement`, `intended_latex_width_fraction`, and `estimated_min_rendered_font_pt`.\n18. Keep figures readable by default: max 4 total panels, max 3 panels per row, max 6 categories per panel, max 4 series per panel, readable fonts, and abbreviated labels instead of tiny text unless the strategy explicitly allows a denser layout.\n19. Choose each LaTeX figure width from the actual generated figure dimensions, aspect ratio, the inspected template geometry, and intended paper footprint. If a figure becomes unreadable at column width, regenerate/simplify it or place it full-width instead of shrinking the same dense figure further.\n20. If `validation_feedback.json` or `validation_feedback.md` is present, treat it as a repair pass: fix every listed hard error first, rerun validation, and keep iterating until the hard errors are gone or the grounded inputs are insufficient.\n21. Do not create visible section or subsection titles centered on `milestones`, `progression`, `timeline`, `phase`, `stage`, `round`, `Experiment A`, `Experiment B`, `first success`, or similar chronology-shaped labels unless the central claim is explicitly about dynamics.\n22. Do not use phrases like `first valid result`, `subsequent improvements`, `next-best`, `later variants`, or `we then tried` in the visible paper unless that wording is strictly necessary inside a brief protocol description.\n23. Keep visible paper text free of internal pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or `rubric`.\n24. If you need a thresholded rate, name it with one reader-facing meaning per figure, such as target attainment rate, successful-run fraction, or non-collapse rate. Do not reuse one label for different task-specific thresholds.\n25. When `paper_data.json.ablation_studies` is non-empty, prefer its variant summaries, replicate statistics, and explicit comparisons over inferring ablations from `all_programs` alone.\n26. Use only numbers present in `paper_data.json`.\n27. Use only citation keys present in `references.bib`.\n28. Use `global_memory_summary.md` only as private qualitative framing; do not use it for quantitative claims or `source_ids`.\n29. If `paper_strategy.md` and `paper_strategy.json` are present, treat them as the framing contract for the final draft; fail clearly if they contradict grounded evidence.\n30. Keep visible paper text free of run-diary wording such as discovery order, iteration order, or agent-loop narration.\n31. Write claim mapping JSON:\n```json\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"text\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\"result.best_correct_score\"],\n      \"citation_keys\": [\"SomeKey2024\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n32. Run validator:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis validator also checks that `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json` stay in sync, and traces every `@evidence[[...]]` link under `--paper-dir` against the `scientific-evidence/` bundle via the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier. Every link must classify as `resolved` or `anchored`; any `dangling` link is a hard error you must fix before finalization. If paper text carries `@evidence[[...]]` tokens but the bundle is missing, generate it first with `bash ../scientific-evidence/scripts/scientific_evidence.sh emit-bundle --root-dir .`.\n33. During repair passes, also rerun the full `validate-paper` workflow after each fix round so the next round starts from fresh feedback, not stale assumptions.\n34. When venue guidance is present, make the title, abstract, introduction, and conclusion explicitly reader-facing for that venue's scientific questions whenever the grounded evidence supports that framing.\n35. If `./paper_authors.tex` exists, treat it as the canonical author source. Keep `\\input{paper_authors.tex}` inside `\\author{...}` when possible; otherwise copy the exact file contents without edits.\n36. Keep the template's watermark block (`% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---` … `% --- end watermark ---`) in `paper.tex` unchanged; the orchestrator regenerates it before every host compile.\n\n# Outputs\n- `paper.tex`\n- `claims_grounding.json`\n- `figure_manifest.json` when figures are generated or required by strategy\n- Optional extra figures in `figures/` (grounded only, no cross-agent aggregate plots).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.json; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat figure_manifest.json; ls scripts; ls scientific-evidence; ls literature; sed -n '1,145p' paper.tex; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat .codex/skills/validate-paper/SKILL.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.json; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.json; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat figure_manifest.json; ls scripts; ls scientific-evidence; ls literature; sed -n '1,145p' paper.tex; cat .agentic_paper_workspace.json\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: validate-paper\ndescription: Validate grounded paper artifacts and compile LaTeX PDF with strict fail-fast checks.\n---\n\n# What the skill is about\nRun final quality gate for the generated paper.\n\n# When to use the skill\n- After `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `claims_grounding.json` are written.\n- Before declaring final paper success.\n\n# What to do\n1. Validate sections and citation keys:\n```bash\npython scripts/validate_tex_refs.py \\\n  --tex paper.tex \\\n  --bib references.bib\n```\nRequire a substantive embedded Supplementary Information section after the bibliography. It must\nuse `\\\\appendix` and `\\\\section{Supplementary Information}`, contain grounded visible content plus\nat least one subsection, table, or figure, and contain no template placeholder text.\n2. Validate claim mappings, bibliography/provenance consistency, and every `@evidence[[...]]` link in the `scientific-evidence/` bundle:\n```bash\npython ../write-paper/scripts/validate_claims.py \\\n  --paper-data paper_data.json \\\n  --references references_provenance.json \\\n  --bib references.bib \\\n  --claims claims_grounding.json \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\nThis delegates to the `scientific-evidence` skill verifier, which classifies every `@evidence[[...]]` link as `resolved`, `anchored`, or `dangling`. Any `dangling` link (or an `@evidence[[...]]` token with no `scientific-evidence/` bundle present) is a hard error and must be sent back to the paper-writing agent for repair.\n3. Validate figure manifest and figure readability budget when `figure_manifest.json` is present or `paper_strategy.json` requires figures. The canonical schema is `templates/figure_manifest.schema.json`.\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py \\\n  --paper-dir .\n```\n4. Compile:\n```bash\nbash scripts/compile_latex.sh paper.tex\n```\nThe compile script also validates that Supplementary Information is visibly rendered on a page\nafter References with substantive content. Source-only structure checks are not sufficient for\nconditional or literal LaTeX constructs.\nIt records and validates every LaTeX input, restricts BibTeX to the canonical `references.bib`,\nand removes inherited Kpathsea path overrides before compiling.\n5. Require `paper.pdf` to exist.\n6. When `paper_data.json.paper_profile == \"standalone\"`, also fail on visible paper language that leaks pipeline terms such as `eligible`, `correct and eligible`, `correctness rate`, `worker_01`, `worker_02`, `combined_score`, or similar internal wording.\n7. When the selected format or venue guidance requires real author metadata, also fail on anonymous or generic placeholder author blocks and emit soft warnings when title/abstract/introduction/conclusion lack venue-relevant framing or when the discussion drifts into unrelated application narratives.\n8. Fail hard on any missing grounding link, missing section, missing/empty/misordered Supplementary Information, unresolved citation, bibliography/provenance mismatch, figure-manifest mismatch, density-budget violation, page-limit violation for formats with enforced budgets, standalone-language violation, author-block violation required by format/venue guidance, dangling `@evidence[[...]]` link, or compile error.\n9. Treat the parent orchestrator's validation report as authoritative during repair rounds. Last-body-page fill is rendered geometric vertical occupancy computed from text and graphics bounds, not character count or word density. Do not claim that page validation passed using a character-count proxy. If the canonical validator is unavailable in the agent workspace, make the targeted repair and return control so the parent can compile and revalidate it.\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"summary\": \"One medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\",\n  \"missing_literatures\": [\n    \"Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation\"\n  ],\n  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n    {\n      \"target_id\": \"method-open-llm-panel-source\",\n      \"section\": \"Method\",\n      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"reason\": \"The opening paragraph of Public score panels and experimental protocol identifies the primary Open LLM six-task contents snapshot without a direct citation to its leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites all six constituent benchmarks, but neither the bibliography nor its provenance includes the source that assembles this panel. Supplementary revision hashes and the local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs, so this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not missing empirical evidence. One canonical official source documenting this suite and its raw-score fields would close it without changing the analysis.\",\n      \"anchor_text\": \"the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates\",\n      \"search_topics\": [\n        \"Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval BBH MATH level 5 GPQA MuSR MMLU-Pro and raw-score fields\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\n\n## Review Basis\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, the 25 entries in `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`, the 57 claim mappings in `claims_grounding.json`, the grounding-source and final-evaluation records in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary supplies qualitative context only. Existing reference provenance limits substantive reading to canonical metadata and abstracts; this memo does not infer stronger support from those records.\n\n## Target: method-open-llm-panel-source\n\n- **Section:** Method, opening paragraph of “Public score panels and experimental protocol” (`paper.tex:97`).\n- **Priority:** medium.\n- **Citation intent:** benchmark_context.\n- **Anchor:** “the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates”.\n- **Missing literature:** Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph names a primary score-panel source without directly citing its official leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro individually, but those sources do not attribute their assembly into this leaderboard panel. No corresponding suite-level source appears among the existing bibliography or provenance entries. The supplementary revision hashes and local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs; this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not a missing-data or unsupported-results problem.\n- **Focused search topic:** Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro, including raw-score fields.\n- **Minimal later change:** Add one verified canonical official source at this paragraph. If available documentation describes a different leaderboard generation or standardized rather than raw scores, do not treat it as support for the pinned panel. Retain the recorded revisions and normalization details as the authority for the actual analyzed inputs. No method or results rewrite is needed.\n\n## Areas Not Flagged\n\n- Related Work directly cites the benchmark families, social-choice aggregation, robust estimation, leakage and contamination, validity, and uncertainty literature it discusses. No uncited named comparator family there warrants an additional retrieval target.\n- Introduction motivation can use the existing benchmark-composition, social-choice, and contamination references if local citation placement is desired; this does not require new references. The discussion of median and mean behavior is explained by the paper's own definitions rather than asserted as an external finding.\n- Exact enumeration, sorted-headroom optimality, monotonicity, and feasible-support bounds are explained and tied to implementation evidence. The paper disclaims optimizer novelty; adding generic optimization citations would not close a demonstrated gap.\n- Quantitative results, cohort definitions, and certificate scope are grounded in the recorded evaluation and claim mappings, not candidates for bibliographic repair. Real training costs and response calibration remain explicit limitations, not invitations to expand the story.\n\n## Retrieval Boundary\n\nThis memo requests only the single source-attribution lookup above, not a citation-count increase or a broader literature pass. Any later lookup must respect the recorded strategy's prohibited-study and no-citation-graph-expansion restrictions. No reference retrieval was performed, and `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` were not modified.\n{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"Conditional minimum task-support investment for a fixed target model to become strictly first under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation with bounded score uplifts; incremental citation work is limited to attribution of the primary Open LLM six-task raw-score panel.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Fixed-rival, bounded score-space intervention with selected-task cardinality as investment; familiar aggregators and exact or bounded support certificates require no new method-literature retrieval.\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Existing citations cover social-choice aggregation, robust estimation, benchmark composition, validity, contamination, and uncertainty; no additional comparator search is justified by the gap artifacts.\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"Primary retrieval target: Open LLM Leaderboard contents snapshot 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2, six raw-score coordinates IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro; acquired 2026-11-17.\",\n    \"HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 and the separately reported 24-subtask BBH results snapshot aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5 are context only, not additional retrieval targets.\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"\\\"Open LLM Leaderboard\\\" IFEval BBH \\\"MATH\\\" \\\"GPQA\\\" \\\"MuSR\\\" \\\"MMLU-Pro\\\" raw scores official documentation\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Close only gap method-open-llm-panel-source (medium priority) at paper.tex:97, Method / Public score panels and experimental protocol, anchored by 'the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates'. The existing bibliography and provenance include IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLUPro component references but no official source attributing their assembly into this panel. Seek one canonical official suite or dataset documentation record establishing both the six-task composition and raw-score field semantics for the recorded contents snapshot 9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2. Verify leaderboard generation and raw versus standardized scores; a different generation or standardized-only description cannot support the pinned raw panel. Supplementary revisions, acquisition records, and normalization remain authoritative for analyzed inputs, not external documentation. If no compatible source is verified, leave this gap unresolved rather than substitute a near-match or broaden retrieval. Add at most one verified source at this paragraph; no method or results rewrite is requested.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"No reference retrieval during this planning stage; all queries are prospective.\",\n    \"Do not identify or discover the prohibited prior study by title, authors, results, paraphrased research question, or broad combinations of targeted investment, leaderboard manipulation, and aggregation rules.\",\n    \"No citation-graph expansion, cited-by searches, related-paper recommendations, reference-list mining, surveys, discussion pages, mirrors, code repositories, or social posts that could expose the prohibited study; later lookup is limited to canonical official suite or primary dataset documentation.\",\n    \"No broad strategic leaderboard or benchmark-cheating discovery; no searches for run-local proposal names as established methods.\",\n    \"No new component-benchmark searches: IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro are already represented in the bibliography and provenance.\",\n    \"No unpinned latest-leaderboard substitution, different-generation suite substitution, standardized-score substitution, Arena detours, or conflation of the six-task contents panel with the separate BBH subtask sensitivity panel.\",\n    \"No generic optimization, robustness, social-choice, contamination, validity, uncertainty, architecture, prompting, or training-cost literature refresh without a demonstrated manuscript gap; no unsupported claims of universal robustness, transfer, or candidate superiority.\",\n    \"Stop after the single attribution target is resolved or found unsupported; do not expand to meet a citation count, rebalance the bibliography, or rewrite methods or results.\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 1 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/1 | \"Open LLM Leaderboard\" IFEval BBH \"MATH\" \"GPQA\" \"MuSR\" \"MMLU-Pro\" raw scores official documentation\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 1/1\n[paper.sh] attempting OpenAlex fallback: Semantic Scholar retrieval did not meet minimum references: 0 < 25\n[paper.sh] OpenAlex contributed 1 references\n{\n  \"figures\": [\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"investment_overview\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"diagram\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/investment_overview.png\",\n      \"n_panels\": 1,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 1,\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        6\n      ],\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3\n      ],\n      \"max_label_chars\": 40,\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": false,\n      \"uses_progression\": false,\n      \"generator\": \"generate-concept-figure\",\n      \"source_ids\": [],\n      \"prompt_log_path\": \"figures/investment_overview.prompts.json\",\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"exact_rule_contrast\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/exact_rule_contrast.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/exact_rule_contrast.png\",\n      \"n_panels\": 2,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        1,\n        1\n      ],\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false,\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9\n    },\n    {\n      \"figure_id\": \"attainability_and_support_scope\",\n      \"figure_type\": \"composite\",\n      \"path_pdf\": \"figures/attainability_and_support_scope.pdf\",\n      \"path_png\": \"figures/attainability_and_support_scope.png\",\n      \"n_panels\": 4,\n      \"panels_per_row\": 2,\n      \"categories_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"series_per_panel\": [\n        3,\n        3,\n        3,\n        3\n      ],\n      \"max_label_chars\": 28,\n      \"font_sizes\": {\n        \"axis\": 10,\n        \"ticks\": 9,\n        \"legend\": 9,\n        \"title\": 10\n      },\n      \"uses_abbrev_labels\": true,\n      \"uses_progression\": false,\n      \"generator\": \"matplotlib\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"placement\": \"full_width\",\n      \"intended_latex_width_fraction\": 1.0,\n      \"estimated_min_rendered_font_pt\": 9\n    }\n  ]\n}\naudit_manuscript.py\nbuild_paper_artifacts.py\ncompile_paper.sh\ncurate_references.py\nfetch_public_inputs.py\nmap_claims.py\nwrite_provenance.py\nbundle.manifest.json\nledger.jsonl\nschemas\nspec.md\nverification-report.json\nBBH.json\nBIGBench.json\nCawley.json\nCheater.json\nCheckList.json\nDatasheets.json\nDemsar.json\nEfron.json\nGPQA.json\nHELM.json\nHuber.json\nIFEval.json\nImageNetReplication.json\nLottery.json\nMATH.json\nMMLU.json\nMMLUPro.json\nModelCards.json\nMuSR.json\nNumPy.json\nRephrased.json\nSciPy.json\nSocialChoice.json\nTruthfulQA.json\nUnderspecification.json\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment\\\\in Multitask Leaderboards}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{K}\n\\newcommand{\\eps}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards compress heterogeneous scores into a single ranking, creating incentives to improve selected tasks. Does replacing the mean with a median make attaining first place more expensive? We study this question under a transparent counterfactual: only a target model changes, each selected score closes a specified fraction of its remaining headroom, and investment counts selected task coordinates. Public HELM and Open LLM score panels permit comparisons of mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean. On fixed, jointly attainable cohorts in the six-task panel, exact minimum-support comparisons reverse between half and full headroom: median-minus-mean support fractions range from $-0.130$ to $-0.069$ at half headroom and from $+0.127$ to $+0.157$ at full headroom across sampling seeds. Attainability also differs substantially across rules. Larger subject panels yield exact mean costs but only bounded costs for order-statistic rules. Thus aggregation resistance is conditional on response ceilings, target selection, and certificate strength. These are score-space sensitivity results, not measured training costs or evidence of real contamination effects.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMultitask benchmarks make model comparison manageable by replacing a vector of task scores with an overall ranking. Their breadth is valuable: knowledge, instruction following, and reasoning need not improve together. But this compression also defines incentives. A developer interested in one model's position may concentrate effort on selected benchmark components rather than improving uniformly. The resulting question is not merely whether rankings change when a task is added or removed. It is how much targeted change to an existing score vector is sufficient to move a fixed target above every rival.\n\nIt is tempting to regard an order-statistic aggregator as intrinsically more resistant to concentrated investment than an arithmetic mean. The mean responds to every coordinate, whereas the median depends on central values and a trimmed mean discards tails. This intuition does not by itself answer the ranking question. Raising a weak score can have substantial effect on the mean but none on the median until the score crosses a central rank. Conversely, a median threshold may be reachable by modifying a relatively small set of central coordinates, while a mean threshold still requires improvement spread across many tasks. The permitted magnitude of each change is therefore part of the question, not an incidental nuisance parameter.\n\nWe formalize investment as the number of task coordinates receiving a bounded target-only uplift. The model is deliberately agnostic about the training intervention that might produce it. It asks a reproducible question about public score geometry with explicit ceilings and fixed competitors. We compare arithmetic mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean on public HELM subject scores and Open LLM task scores. A separate BBH subtask sensitivity check is kept distinct from the primary panels. These are different resolutions of evaluation, not interchangeable units of real-world training expense.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n\nOur central observation is a conditional ordering reversal. Holding the target cohort fixed within each sampling seed, the median requires less support on average than the mean at half headroom, but more at full headroom. This comparison uses exact costs on the six-task panel. It is not inferred from a mixture of successful and unsuccessful targets, or from heuristic solutions on larger panels. Alongside this contrast, we report all-target attainability, support intervals where exactness is unavailable, and matched selection-policy comparisons.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nThe contributions are an explicit, implementable investment definition; an exact matched-cohort demonstration of response-dependent aggregation ordering; and an evidence-strength-aware comparison that separates attainability, minimum costs, and feasible upper bounds. We do not propose a new optimizer or infer an isolated component effect from uncontrolled changes. The main implication is methodological: an aggregation rule should not be described as resistant to targeted investment without specifying the response model and which cost statements are actually certified.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/investment_overview.png}\n\\caption{The studied counterfactual: modify only selected target coordinates, hold rivals fixed, and compare aggregation conditions. The outcome is an exact minimum support, certified unattainability, or a lower--upper support interval. No model training is represented.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and benchmark composition.}\nMMLU evaluates knowledge across subjects \\citep{MMLU}; HELM emphasizes broad, transparent evaluation across scenarios and metrics \\citep{HELM}. BIG-bench and BBH characterize diverse tasks and difficult reasoning settings \\citep{BIGBench,BBH}. The six-task panel studied here combines instruction following \\citep{IFEval}, challenging mathematics \\citep{MATH}, expert question answering \\citep{GPQA}, narrative reasoning \\citep{MuSR}, and knowledge-oriented reasoning \\citep{MMLUPro}, alongside BBH. These source works explain the tasks' purposes; our empirical claims concern the pinned score snapshots, not a replication of their original experiments. The benchmark-lottery perspective emphasizes that relative performance depends on benchmark choices \\citep{Lottery}. We instead hold task membership fixed in the principal comparison and vary the aggregation rule and allowed target response.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation and statistical robustness.}\n\\citet{SocialChoice} interpret tasks as voters and evaluation as social aggregation. Our comparison uses cardinal score summaries rather than implementing their ordinal voting framework. Classical robust estimation studies sensitivity to contamination of observations \\citep{Huber}. Resistance of a location estimate to contamination is not the same as support needed to exceed a rival's aggregation threshold under bounded upward changes. In particular, the target's order statistics and the rival threshold both matter here. We make no claim that the familiar mean, median, trimmed mean, exhaustive enumeration, or greedy policies are new.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\nBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\nComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nAll selected coordinates receive the same fraction of remaining headroom. Unselected scores and all rival scores are unchanged. The evaluated ceilings are $\\alpha\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Full headroom allows a selected score to reach one; it is an optimistic stress condition, not an empirical prediction. Let $A_r$ denote aggregation rule $r$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n b_r=\\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(X_i),\\qquad\n \\cost_r(x,\\alpha)=\\min_S\\{|S|:A_r(x^{S,\\alpha})>b_r+\\eps\\},\n \\label{eq:cost}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\eps=10^{-10}$ implements strict first place. If no support wins, $\\cost_r=\\infty$. The normalized investment is $\\cost_r/m$ when attainable. A tie does not count as first place.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\nFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n\nThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n\nWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n\n\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\nThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n\nFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\nThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n\nModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n\nThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\nMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\nThe central comparison keeps a fixed cohort per seed across both rules and both ceilings. The three-rule support plots use the within-ceiling joint cohort, which may differ across ceilings; seed means receive equal weight. We show individual seed summaries rather than inventing a population confidence interval. Existing policy intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with resampling seed 48271 and percentile endpoints at 95\\%. They condition on the fixed snapshots and rival pools. They do not test differences between aggregation rules. Overlapping targets or related models across seeds further preclude treating these as independent training replications.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L1046-L1068]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#paired_intervals]]\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Exact investment ordering changes with the response ceiling}\nOn the primary six-task panel, the fixed cohorts jointly attainable under mean and median at half and full headroom contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019, respectively. Figure~\\ref{fig:exact} shows the paired difference\n\\begin{equation}\n D_s(\\alpha)=\\frac{1}{|C_s|}\\sum_{t\\in C_s}\n \\frac{\\cost_{\\mathrm{median}}(X_t,\\alpha)-\\cost_{\\mathrm{mean}}(X_t,\\alpha)}{6}.\n\\end{equation}\nThe cohort $C_s$ is unchanged between panels. All included supports have exact minimum status backed by exhaustive selection. Therefore the comparison is about minimum investment in the specified model, not differences in heuristic success.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/exact_rule_contrast.pdf}\n\\caption{Exact paired contrast on fixed Open LLM cohorts. Negative values mean the median requires fewer modified coordinates on average. The same targets are retained at half and full headroom within each seed; cohort sizes are 18, 17, and 17. Points are descriptive seed summaries, not confidence intervals.}\n\\label{fig:exact}\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nAt half headroom, $D_s$ is $-0.1296$, $-0.1275$, and $-0.0686$ in seed order. At full headroom it is $+0.1389$, $+0.1275$, and $+0.1569$. Thus the average ordering reverses in every sampled cohort: median support is lower at half headroom and higher at full headroom. This does not mean each target reverses, nor does it estimate how frequently reversal would occur in a new leaderboard. The contrast was selected descriptively during analysis of the recorded results and is not a preregistered hypothesis test.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nHolding the cohort fixed is important. At half headroom, some targets cannot win under one or both rules. Comparing the mean on all mean-attainable targets with the median on all median-attainable targets would conflate ranking resistance with selection of different models. Our fixed-cohort comparison removes that particular change in composition, while remaining conditional on joint attainability. Its claim is consequently narrower, but also cleaner: response ceilings can change rule ordering even without changing the compared targets.\n\n\\subsection{Attainability and support answer different questions}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:scope} includes all ordinary primary targets in its attainment panels. Each rule--ceiling cell contains 72 target decisions across the sampling seeds. On Open LLM, quarter headroom yields attainment for 11 targets under the mean, one under the median, and eight under trimming. At half headroom these counts are 65, 52, and 67; full headroom attains all 72 under every rule. A median can therefore have lower average cost on the jointly attainable cohort while excluding more targets overall. Neither quantity should be substituted for the other.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#grid]]\n\n{\n  \"global_memory_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"output_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/paper\",\n  \"results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"task_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/task\"\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
