{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls -l scripts .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; sed -n '1,105p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,112p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"ls -l scripts .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts .codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts; cat postdraft_reference_retrieval.log; cat postdraft_reference_query_plan.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.md; sed -n '1,105p' paper_strategy.md; sed -n '1,112p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":".codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts:\ntotal 52\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 agent agent  4096 Nov 17 14:07 __pycache__\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 agent agent  1710 Nov 16 18:22 compile_latex.sh\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 11625 Nov 16 18:22 validate_compile_inputs.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  2742 Nov 16 18:22 validate_figures.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  4293 Nov 16 18:22 validate_rendered_si.py\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 agent agent 17587 Nov 16 18:22 validate_tex_refs.py\n\n.codex/skills/write-paper-standalone/scripts:\ntotal 28\n-rwxr-xr-x 1 agent agent 26915 Nov 16 18:22 validate_claims.py\n\nscripts:\ntotal 56\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  6166 Nov 17 14:07 audit_manuscript.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 16380 Nov 17 14:03 build_paper_artifacts.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   313 Nov 17 14:08 compile_paper.sh\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  5677 Nov 17 14:00 curate_references.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  1093 Nov 17 14:02 fetch_public_inputs.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  9069 Nov 17 14:03 map_claims.py\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  1942 Nov 17 14:08 write_provenance.py\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 1 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/1 | \"Open LLM Leaderboard\" IFEval BBH \"MATH\" \"GPQA\" \"MuSR\" \"MMLU-Pro\" raw scores official documentation\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/25 (25 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 1/1\n[paper.sh] attempting OpenAlex fallback: Semantic Scholar retrieval did not meet minimum references: 0 < 25\n[paper.sh] OpenAlex contributed 1 references\n# Post-draft Reference Query Plan\n\n**Status:** ready  \n**Scope:** one medium-priority benchmark-source attribution query; no retrieval performed.\n\n## Central Topic and Grounding\n\nThe manuscript studies conditional minimum task-support investment for a fixed target model to become strictly first under mean, median, and trimmed-mean aggregation with bounded score uplifts. This incremental plan concerns only attribution of its primary Open LLM six-task raw-score panel.\n\nRead `draft_reference_gaps.md` and `draft_reference_gaps.json` first, then checked the draft, existing bibliography and provenance, global memory summary, and original query-plan structure. Both gap artifacts identify only `method-open-llm-panel-source`. One query, rather than the default three to six, respects their explicit single-lookup boundary. No full literature refresh or bibliography rebalancing is justified.\n\nExisting component citations are `IFEval`, `BBH`, `MATH`, `GPQA`, `MuSR`, and `MMLUPro`. The 25 bibliography/provenance entries contain no suite-level official Open LLM source. Provenance records report canonical metadata and abstract reading only; this plan does not infer deeper source support. Existing related-work citations cover social choice, robust estimation, benchmark composition, contamination, validity, and uncertainty. No new comparator or method-family search is requested. There are no architectural-shift or result-driven retrieval targets.\n\n## Query 1: Primary Panel Attribution\n\n- **Category:** `benchmark_context`.\n- **Priority and target:** medium; `method-open-llm-panel-source`.\n- **Location:** `paper.tex:97`, Method, opening paragraph of “Public score panels and experimental protocol”.\n- **Anchor:** “the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates”.\n- **Query:** `\"Open LLM Leaderboard\" IFEval BBH \"MATH\" \"GPQA\" \"MuSR\" \"MMLU-Pro\" raw scores official documentation`\n\n**Rationale:** Constituent benchmark papers explain individual tasks but do not attribute their assembly into the leaderboard panel. A single canonical official suite or primary dataset documentation source should establish the six-task composition and raw-score field semantics compatible with contents snapshot `9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2`, acquired on 2026-11-17. This is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not missing empirical evidence.\n\n**Acceptance and stopping rule:** Later verification must distinguish leaderboard generations and raw versus standardized scores. A source describing a different generation or only standardized scores is not support for the pinned raw panel. Recorded revisions, acquisition records, and supplementary normalization details remain authoritative for the actual analyzed inputs. Add at most one verified compatible source at this paragraph; no method or results rewrite is needed. If no compatible official source is verified, leave the gap explicitly unresolved rather than substitute a near-match or broaden retrieval.\n\n## Boundaries and Deferred Work\n\n- This stage plans only: it retrieves no references and changes neither `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, nor `references_provenance.json`.\n- Preserve prohibited-study restrictions: no discovery by title, authors, results, paraphrased question, or broad strategic-leaderboard/benchmark-cheating searches. Do not search run-local proposal names as established methods.\n- No citation-graph expansion, cited-by searches, recommendations, reference-list mining, surveys, discussions, mirrors, code repositories, or social posts that could expose the prohibited study. Limit later lookup to canonical official suite or primary dataset documentation.\n- Do not repeat component-benchmark searches, substitute an unpinned latest leaderboard, use standardized-only descriptions, detour into Arena, or conflate the six-task contents panel with the separate 24-subtask BBH sensitivity panel. HELM MMLU v1.13.0 and BBH results snapshot `aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5` are context, not new targets.\n- No generic optimization, robustness, social-choice, contamination, validity, uncertainty, architecture, prompting, or training-cost literature refresh. Do not introduce claims of universal robustness, transfer, or candidate superiority.\n- Stop after the single attribution target is resolved or found unsupported; do not expand for citation count, bibliography balance, or methods/results rewriting.\n\nThe companion JSON retains the field structure of `reference_query_plan.json`; its sole prospective query mirrors this memo.\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\n\n## Review Basis\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, the 25 entries in `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`, the 57 claim mappings in `claims_grounding.json`, the grounding-source and final-evaluation records in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary supplies qualitative context only. Existing reference provenance limits substantive reading to canonical metadata and abstracts; this memo does not infer stronger support from those records.\n\n## Target: method-open-llm-panel-source\n\n- **Section:** Method, opening paragraph of “Public score panels and experimental protocol” (`paper.tex:97`).\n- **Priority:** medium.\n- **Citation intent:** benchmark_context.\n- **Anchor:** “the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates”.\n- **Missing literature:** Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph names a primary score-panel source without directly citing its official leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro individually, but those sources do not attribute their assembly into this leaderboard panel. No corresponding suite-level source appears among the existing bibliography or provenance entries. The supplementary revision hashes and local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs; this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not a missing-data or unsupported-results problem.\n- **Focused search topic:** Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro, including raw-score fields.\n- **Minimal later change:** Add one verified canonical official source at this paragraph. If available documentation describes a different leaderboard generation or standardized rather than raw scores, do not treat it as support for the pinned panel. Retain the recorded revisions and normalization details as the authority for the actual analyzed inputs. No method or results rewrite is needed.\n\n## Areas Not Flagged\n\n- Related Work directly cites the benchmark families, social-choice aggregation, robust estimation, leakage and contamination, validity, and uncertainty literature it discusses. No uncited named comparator family there warrants an additional retrieval target.\n- Introduction motivation can use the existing benchmark-composition, social-choice, and contamination references if local citation placement is desired; this does not require new references. The discussion of median and mean behavior is explained by the paper's own definitions rather than asserted as an external finding.\n- Exact enumeration, sorted-headroom optimality, monotonicity, and feasible-support bounds are explained and tied to implementation evidence. The paper disclaims optimizer novelty; adding generic optimization citations would not close a demonstrated gap.\n- Quantitative results, cohort definitions, and certificate scope are grounded in the recorded evaluation and claim mappings, not candidates for bibliographic repair. Real training costs and response calibration remain explicit limitations, not invitations to expand the story.\n\n## Retrieval Boundary\n\nThis memo requests only the single source-attribution lookup above, not a citation-count increase or a broader literature pass. Any later lookup must respect the recorded strategy's prohibited-study and no-citation-graph-expansion restrictions. No reference retrieval was performed, and `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` were not modified.\n# Paper strategy: response ceilings and targeted investment\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.\n**Provisional title:** Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards.\n\n## Scientific center\n\n**Question.** Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\n\nThis is a standalone conditional empirical study, not an optimizer breakthrough, a training experiment, or a search report. Organize the six-task and subject-level panels as complementary evidence blocks under one question. Their certificate strength differs; neither is evidence of architecture change or domain transfer. The contribution is the precisely defined investment comparison and its measured limits, not a claim that familiar enumeration or greedy selection is new.\n\n## Framing decision and rejected alternatives\n\nNo edit-context file, previous manuscript, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an assumed inherited improvement narrative; there is no actual prior manuscript to reject.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an optimizer-improvement story | Reject: the frozen selection is the reference baseline, not a successful new optimizer. |\n| Broaden to a shared novel certificate family | Reject: no component ablations substantiate the abandoned proposal mechanisms, and large-panel median/trimmed costs are not exact. |\n| Recenter on response-dependent investment | Choose: final ordinary decision records support direct rule-by-ceiling comparisons, including exact paired contrasts and explicit unattainability. |\n\nDirect inspection confirms that the staged selected and baseline source files are identical. Their small-panel enumeration, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses determine what can be claimed. The auxiliary projections and BBH records provide additional scope checks, not a reason to broaden the thesis to a new method family. The venue changes presentation, not the scientific conclusion.\n\n## Private grounding and qualification audit\n\nThis section is a provenance memo, not manuscript prose. Use these registered source identifiers in downstream claim mapping:\n\n- `final.qualification.status = completed`, `final.qualification.outcome = qualified`, and `final.qualification.correct = true`. These come from `paper_data.json.final_evaluation`, read independently of selection metadata. Qualification does not establish novelty, population generalization, or every support minimum.\n- `study.selection_kind = baseline`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`; `study.attempts.scored = 3`; `study.attempts.unscored = 0`. Search evidence and final measurements have distinct scopes. No search alternative has an established qualifying improvement. Missing performance evidence would not be a measured loss.\n- `final.evidence.episodes` contains three sampling seeds, ordinary and matched-control cases, primary and auxiliary decisions, and policy-comparison intervals. Its `candidate_diagnostics_trust` remains `unverified`. The evidence-file SHA-256 agrees with the manifest. Direct evaluator inspection shows witness verification and exhaustive small-panel reference computation; that does not license unrelated candidate diagnostics. The field `exact_audit_pass` alone is not proof of full-panel exactness.\n- Preserve the final qualification assessment through writing, review, audit, and revision. If authoritative later evidence changes it to a failure, disclose that failure explicitly and retain its limitations. Failed search alternatives have no established causal diagnosis: do not infer performance-only, validity, or adaptivity failure from a boolean outcome.\n\nThe cross-rule cohort check was a read-only summary of existing records, not a new experiment. Restrict to ordinary primary Open LLM decisions and join on seed and target slot. Intersect targets attainable under both mean and median at both half and full headroom. The resulting fixed cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. The following rounded values are audit checks derived from `final.evidence.episodes`, not hard-coded plotting inputs:\n\n| Sampling seed | Median minus mean support fraction, half headroom | Same contrast, full headroom |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1009 | -0.129630 | +0.138889 |\n| 1013 | -0.127451 | +0.127451 |\n| 1019 | -0.068627 | +0.156863 |\n\nAll feasible primary six-task costs in this contrast have exact support status and correspond to the frozen exhaustive procedure. The reversal is a descriptive within-snapshot result selected during framing, not a preregistered significance finding. Larger-panel HELM witness-cost reversals are not minimum-cost reversals when certification intervals overlap.\n\n## Scope and method groups\n\nDefine scores in `[0,1]`, a target model, a fixed rival pool, a task panel of size `m`, and selected support `S`. Only selected coordinates change, by `x_j -> x_j + alpha(1-x_j)`, with recorded `alpha` values `0.25`, `0.5`, and `1.0`. Unique first means target aggregate exceeds the largest rival aggregate by more than `1e-10`. Investment is `|S|`, with `|S|/m` used for normalized display. Total normalized score lift is secondary, not dollars, training examples, or compute.\n\nInclude only these method groups:\n\n1. **Frozen support-minimization procedure — supporting.** Exhaust at most six coordinates, sort headroom for larger means, and construct marginal-score-greedy supports for larger median/trimmed panels. Explain the all-coordinate infeasibility test and exact versus lower–upper outcomes.\n2. **Equal-task arithmetic mean — aggregation baseline.** Retain throughout; the headroom solution is exact.\n3. **Conventional median — supporting condition.** Average the two middle scores on even panels; do not substitute a different median convention.\n4. **Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean — supporting condition.** Remove `floor(m/5)` scores from each tail.\n5. **No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy policies — selection baselines.** Keep matched recorded comparisons, including ties. They are not component ablations.\n\nDo not require discarded rank-window or clipped-envelope branches in the main narrative. No evaluated Borda condition, equilibrium model, measured training response, architecture intervention, or unrun ablation belongs in this paper. State equal task weights, no spillover, no score regression outside the support, fixed rivals, and uncertain mapping from score uplift to actual training investment.\n\n## Results structure and uncertainty\n\nLead with the exact fixed-cohort mean–median contrast. Follow it with all-target attainability and support/certificate scope for both primary families. Maintain one definition of **target attainment rate**: the fraction of ordinary primary target decisions with a verified strict-first support and a supported claim. Do not mix controls into that rate.\n\nUse a compact main comparator table at half headroom for both families and all three rules, retaining ties with greedy and comparisons against random and no intervention. The complete rule/ceiling/seed grid and all recorded comparator intervals belong after references. A meaningful exact-selection advantage in one small-panel condition does not make the frozen reference a newly invented optimizer.\n\nThe main body must distinguish:\n\n- Exact six-task and mean minimum costs from larger median/trimmed feasible upper bounds and verified lower bounds.\n- Unattainable cases from unresolved support intervals. Never draw the `m+1` infeasibility sentinel as an actual intervention cost.\n- Successful-target support from a penalized effort summary: `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction` assigns one to nonattainment. Likewise, `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` measures distance to a verified lower bound, not necessarily true optimizer error.\n- Paired comparisons on identical targets from cross-ceiling summaries with different attainable cohorts.\n- Three sampling seeds from independent training replicates. Public pools and some targets/ancestry may overlap.\n\nExisting intervals use 200 paired cluster resamples with RNG seed 48271, conditional on fixed snapshots and rival pools. They compare the selected procedure with random or greedy policies, not aggregation rules with each other. Do not reuse those intervals for the central reversal, invent p-values, or call the certification intervals statistical confidence intervals. Show all seed summaries and describe the cross-rule comparison as descriptive.\n\nKeep matched constant-ceiling, zero-response, permutation, and replay checks in a concise scope discussion; replay has statistical weight zero. Passing them establishes only the tested properties. Report auxiliary six-coordinate projections and revision-matched BBH separately, never pooled into primary scores or described as independent transfer replication.\n\n## Required figures and layout\n\nThe inspected `paper.sty` declares a **5.5-inch text width and 9-inch text height** in a single-column ICLR layout. Thus `full_width` means the one text column, not a two-column spread. Half-width placement would provide only about 2.75 inches and is unsuitable for the planned multi-panel comparisons. Use ordinary full-text-width figures, not a two-column layout workaround.\n\n| Figure | Concrete design and source | Readability budget and footprint |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1. Investment overview | `generate-concept-figure`; fixed scores/rivals, target-only headroom equation, three aggregation branches, exact/infeasible/bounded support outcomes. Source: `final.evidence.episodes`, interpreted with inspected source/contract. No numerical toy result. | One shallow diagram, at most six visual groups and three aggregator branches; approximately 5.5 by 1.8 inches; full width on page 1 or 2; at least 9-point labels. |\n| 2. Exact rule contrast | `matplotlib`; two side-by-side signed dot plots at half and full headroom, each showing the per-seed median-minus-mean exact support fraction on the same fixed cohort. Source: ordinary primary Open LLM slices of `final.evidence.episodes`. | Two panels, two per row, three seed categories, one contrast series; approximately 5.5 by 2.1 inches; full width; same signed y scale and zero line; caption gives cohort counts. No invented error bars. |\n| 3. Attainability and support scope | `matplotlib`; 2-by-2 composite, rows HELM/Open LLM; left all-target attainment at three ceilings; right paired support bounds at half/full headroom. Source: ordinary primary `final.evidence.episodes`. | Four panels, two per row, at most three categories and three rule series per panel; approximately 5.5 by 3.4 inches; full width, common legend and readable scales; at least 9-point labels. |\n\nFor Figure 3 right panels, intersect attainable targets across all three rules within each seed and ceiling, state cohort sizes, and average the within-seed lower and feasible upper fractions with declared equal seed weights. Unlike Figure 2, these cohorts may differ across ceilings. Exact costs collapse to points; other intervals are certification ranges. Show an empty cohort as not estimable, not as a cost or an imputed value. Do not use these changing-cohort panels alone to establish the central reversal.\n\nAll figures are direct condition comparisons; no optimization trajectories, milestone views, or campaign timelines. Use tables for scope, status counts, and policy differences rather than another dense dashboard. Quantitative figures must export vector PDF and PNG at 300 DPI from source; LaTeX uses PDF whenever available. Preserve editable overview source and prompt provenance. If only raster is available, regenerate at least 2400 pixels wide for full-width placement, or 1200 pixels for a later justified narrow placement; never upscale. Only the overview uses the concept-figure workflow. This stage does not generate figures or invoke that workflow.\n\n## Main-body budget and venue contract\n\nTarget ICLR 2026, anonymized, with about **8.5 substantive body pages excluding references**. Figure area is included in the following allocation:\n\n| Scientific use of space | Approximate pages |\n| --- | ---: |\n| Abstract, introduction, precise contribution and scope | 1.25 |\n| Safely grounded related work and modeling context | 0.75 |\n| Bounded-response objective and assumptions | 1.00 |\n| Support computation and certificate scope | 1.00 |\n| Public-panel protocol, pairing and uncertainty | 0.75 |\n| Exact contrast, attainment, support bounds and comparators, including figures | 2.75 |\n| Limitations and conclusion | 1.00 |\n\nAn eight-page body counts only if its last page is at least 75 percent full. Expand useful model explanation, certificate reasoning, comparison clarity, and limitation analysis before adding visual or textual filler. Do not import the NeurIPS template or checklist obligation from a source-data manifest in place of the selected ICLR venue requirements. Use the provided ICLR style, with no author names, affiliations, identifying links, acknowledgements, or funding statement. Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, optionally after references, describing actual assistance only.\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment\\\\in Multitask Leaderboards}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{K}\n\\newcommand{\\eps}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards compress heterogeneous scores into a single ranking, creating incentives to improve selected tasks. Does replacing the mean with a median make attaining first place more expensive? We study this question under a transparent counterfactual: only a target model changes, each selected score closes a specified fraction of its remaining headroom, and investment counts selected task coordinates. Public HELM and Open LLM score panels permit comparisons of mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean. On fixed, jointly attainable cohorts in the six-task panel, exact minimum-support comparisons reverse between half and full headroom: median-minus-mean support fractions range from $-0.130$ to $-0.069$ at half headroom and from $+0.127$ to $+0.157$ at full headroom across sampling seeds. Attainability also differs substantially across rules. Larger subject panels yield exact mean costs but only bounded costs for order-statistic rules. Thus aggregation resistance is conditional on response ceilings, target selection, and certificate strength. These are score-space sensitivity results, not measured training costs or evidence of real contamination effects.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMultitask benchmarks make model comparison manageable by replacing a vector of task scores with an overall ranking. Their breadth is valuable: knowledge, instruction following, and reasoning need not improve together. But this compression also defines incentives. A developer interested in one model's position may concentrate effort on selected benchmark components rather than improving uniformly. The resulting question is not merely whether rankings change when a task is added or removed. It is how much targeted change to an existing score vector is sufficient to move a fixed target above every rival.\n\nIt is tempting to regard an order-statistic aggregator as intrinsically more resistant to concentrated investment than an arithmetic mean. The mean responds to every coordinate, whereas the median depends on central values and a trimmed mean discards tails. This intuition does not by itself answer the ranking question. Raising a weak score can have substantial effect on the mean but none on the median until the score crosses a central rank. Conversely, a median threshold may be reachable by modifying a relatively small set of central coordinates, while a mean threshold still requires improvement spread across many tasks. The permitted magnitude of each change is therefore part of the question, not an incidental nuisance parameter.\n\nWe formalize investment as the number of task coordinates receiving a bounded target-only uplift. The model is deliberately agnostic about the training intervention that might produce it. It asks a reproducible question about public score geometry with explicit ceilings and fixed competitors. We compare arithmetic mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean on public HELM subject scores and Open LLM task scores. A separate BBH subtask sensitivity check is kept distinct from the primary panels. These are different resolutions of evaluation, not interchangeable units of real-world training expense.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n\nOur central observation is a conditional ordering reversal. Holding the target cohort fixed within each sampling seed, the median requires less support on average than the mean at half headroom, but more at full headroom. This comparison uses exact costs on the six-task panel. It is not inferred from a mixture of successful and unsuccessful targets, or from heuristic solutions on larger panels. Alongside this contrast, we report all-target attainability, support intervals where exactness is unavailable, and matched selection-policy comparisons.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nThe contributions are an explicit, implementable investment definition; an exact matched-cohort demonstration of response-dependent aggregation ordering; and an evidence-strength-aware comparison that separates attainability, minimum costs, and feasible upper bounds. We do not propose a new optimizer or infer an isolated component effect from uncontrolled changes. The main implication is methodological: an aggregation rule should not be described as resistant to targeted investment without specifying the response model and which cost statements are actually certified.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/investment_overview.png}\n\\caption{The studied counterfactual: modify only selected target coordinates, hold rivals fixed, and compare aggregation conditions. The outcome is an exact minimum support, certified unattainability, or a lower--upper support interval. No model training is represented.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Evaluation breadth and benchmark composition.}\nMMLU evaluates knowledge across subjects \\citep{MMLU}; HELM emphasizes broad, transparent evaluation across scenarios and metrics \\citep{HELM}. BIG-bench and BBH characterize diverse tasks and difficult reasoning settings \\citep{BIGBench,BBH}. The six-task panel studied here combines instruction following \\citep{IFEval}, challenging mathematics \\citep{MATH}, expert question answering \\citep{GPQA}, narrative reasoning \\citep{MuSR}, and knowledge-oriented reasoning \\citep{MMLUPro}, alongside BBH. These source works explain the tasks' purposes; our empirical claims concern the pinned score snapshots, not a replication of their original experiments. The benchmark-lottery perspective emphasizes that relative performance depends on benchmark choices \\citep{Lottery}. We instead hold task membership fixed in the principal comparison and vary the aggregation rule and allowed target response.\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation and statistical robustness.}\n\\citet{SocialChoice} interpret tasks as voters and evaluation as social aggregation. Our comparison uses cardinal score summaries rather than implementing their ordinal voting framework. Classical robust estimation studies sensitivity to contamination of observations \\citep{Huber}. Resistance of a location estimate to contamination is not the same as support needed to exceed a rival's aggregation threshold under bounded upward changes. In particular, the target's order statistics and the rival threshold both matter here. We make no claim that the familiar mean, median, trimmed mean, exhaustive enumeration, or greedy policies are new.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity beyond a published score.}\nBenchmark leakage and rephrased contamination motivate taking strategic score changes seriously \\citep{Cheater,Rephrased}, but do not establish our assumed response law. Behavioral testing \\citep{CheckList}, truthfulness evaluation \\citep{TruthfulQA}, replication on fresh test sets \\citep{ImageNetReplication}, and underspecification \\citep{Underspecification} each illustrate a distinct reason not to equate one aggregate with general capability. Dataset and model documentation support stating intended uses and limitations \\citep{Datasheets,ModelCards}. We follow that principle by distinguishing a hypothetical score intervention from observed training.\n\n\\paragraph{Comparisons and uncertainty.}\nComparisons across datasets require attention to the experimental unit \\citep{Demsar}, and selection itself can bias performance evaluation \\citep{Cawley}. Accordingly, our cross-rule reversal is descriptive and conditional on the observed snapshots and a post-selected framing. Recorded bootstrap intervals \\citep{Efron} concern paired selection policies, not the reversal hypothesis. The reproduction environment specifies NumPy and SciPy \\citep{NumPy,SciPy}; the summaries use NumPy arrays.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt]]\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem setup and response model}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{n\\times m}$ be a public score matrix, with one row per evaluated model and one coordinate per task. Fix a target row $t$, its score vector $x=X_t$, and all other rows as rivals. For support $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and headroom fraction $\\alpha$, define\n\\begin{equation}\n x^{S,\\alpha}_j=x_j+\\alpha(1-x_j)\\,\\mathbf{1}\\{j\\in S\\}.\n \\label{eq:response}\n\\end{equation}\nAll selected coordinates receive the same fraction of remaining headroom. Unselected scores and all rival scores are unchanged. The evaluated ceilings are $\\alpha\\in\\{0.25,0.5,1\\}$. Full headroom allows a selected score to reach one; it is an optimistic stress condition, not an empirical prediction. Let $A_r$ denote aggregation rule $r$ and\n\\begin{equation}\n b_r=\\max_{i\\ne t} A_r(X_i),\\qquad\n \\cost_r(x,\\alpha)=\\min_S\\{|S|:A_r(x^{S,\\alpha})>b_r+\\eps\\},\n \\label{eq:cost}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $\\eps=10^{-10}$ implements strict first place. If no support wins, $\\cost_r=\\infty$. The normalized investment is $\\cost_r/m$ when attainable. A tie does not count as first place.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n\nThis is an equal-coordinate cost model: intervening on a broad evaluation task and on a narrow subject each costs one within its own panel. Consequently, normalized support compares concentration within panels, not absolute work across datasets. It does not measure examples, money, accelerator time, or achievable learning gains. The assumptions exclude spillover, negative transfer, rivalry responses, and heterogeneity in the amount of work required to close headroom. Total score lift is available as a secondary diagnostic, but cannot calibrate those omitted quantities.\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation conditions and design rationale}\nFor sorted target values $z_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le z_{(m)}$, the mean is $m^{-1}\\sum_j z_j$. The conventional median takes the central order statistic for odd $m$ and averages the central pair for even $m$. The trimmed mean removes $q=\\lfloor m/5\\rfloor$ values from each end and averages the remaining $m-2q$. Tasks otherwise have equal weight. In the six-task panel this removes one value at each end, whereas on the subject panel trimming removes eleven at each end.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L70-L79]]\n\nThe research question is whether the relative minimum support for two rules changes with $\\alpha$. The mechanistic rationale is rank dependence: the mean rewards every positive uplift immediately; median and trimming rewards depend on where altered coordinates fall after sorting. Changing $\\alpha$ changes which ranks a coordinate can reach. It also changes each rule's attainable set. There is therefore no reason to interpret a difference computed only on currently successful targets as a pure cost difference without controlling the cohort. We assess response-dependent ordering directly, without attributing it to a new selection component.\n\nWithin any fixed rule and target, increasing $\\alpha$ cannot worsen attainability or increase the true minimum support: every coordinate and all three aggregators are monotone. This property does not constrain the sign of the difference between two rules' costs. A reversal of that difference is compatible with falling costs under both rules. Our primary contrast is the average median-minus-mean normalized support on targets attainable under both rules at both compared ceilings.\n\n\\subsection{Exact solutions, feasible witnesses, and bounds}\nThe frozen procedure begins by testing the full support. Since every allowed change is nonnegative and aggregation is coordinatewise monotone, failure of the full support certifies unattainability. This certificate is distinct from failure of a limited search. For a panel of at most six coordinates, supports are enumerated by cardinality, with deterministic semantic task-ID ordering. The first winning cardinality is exact. Thus every attainable primary six-task result used for the central contrast is a minimum-support result, not merely a successful heuristic plan.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L99-L117]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\nFor the mean on larger panels, sort headrooms $h_j=1-x_j$ from largest to smallest. Writing $h_{[j]}$ for this ordering, the minimum is the smallest $k$ satisfying\n\\begin{equation}\n \\frac{1}{m}\\sum_j x_j+\\frac{\\alpha}{m}\\sum_{j=1}^k h_{[j]}>b_{\\mathrm{mean}}+\\eps.\n \\label{eq:mean}\n\\end{equation}\nNo size-$k$ set can have greater summed headroom than this prefix, so a shorter failing prefix excludes all shorter supports. This explains exactness without invoking an unobserved solver behavior. Ties are resolved by semantic task identity.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L118-L125]]\n\nFor larger median and trimmed panels, the procedure greedily adds the coordinate maximizing the immediate updated aggregate, again breaking ties by semantic task identity. It stops at a verified strict-first support. Its size is an upper bound $U$, not necessarily $\\cost_r$. Independently computed conservative lower bounds $L$ yield $L\\le\\cost_r\\le U$. The reported $L$ is the evaluator's verified reference bound, not a claim that the greedy procedure computes an exact optimum. Mean costs and exhaustive small-panel costs collapse to points. Unattainable cases have no finite support interval; the machine-readable out-of-range sentinel is never plotted as an investment cost.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126-L137]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L761-L789]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L873-L925]]\n\n\\subsection{Public score panels and experimental protocol}\nThe primary panels are HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 with 57 subject coordinates and the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates: IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro. The snapshots were acquired on 2026-11-17. The extracted records preserve original rows and settings; completeness and finite scores in the unit interval are required. These scores are not standardized effect sizes or observations from a homogeneous new evaluation. Source versions, checksums, filtering, and normalization are recorded in the supplementary reproduction specification.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/records.json]]\n\nModel aliases, normalized raw components, and score-map identities are grouped before partitioning the source records. The final evaluation uses a frozen, content-separated partition rather than a newly trained model. Within each family, rows tied for the lead under any studied rule are excluded from target selection. Remaining rows are sorted by mean score and divided into 24 bands; one target per band is sampled using each seed in $\\{1009,1013,1019\\}$. All rivals in the corresponding frozen pool remain visible. Each target is evaluated under every rule and ceiling, providing paired decisions rather than independently sampled rule-specific targets. Seeds vary target sampling, not model training.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L614-L687]]\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#seeds]]\n\nThe evaluator checks returned supports against the supplied matrix and accepts exactness only when independently supported. The ordinary primary cases define the reported target attainment rate: a verified strict-first support with a supported claim, divided by all primary targets in that cell. Constant-ceiling, zero-response, joint column-permutation, and reset-replay checks test specific consistency properties. Replay carries no statistical weight. The completed frozen evaluation satisfies its recorded qualification requirements; this does not certify generalization outside the snapshots or validate unverified auxiliary diagnostics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/data/manifest.json#protocol_policy]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/status]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/outcome]]\n\n\\subsection{Comparators, conditioning, and uncertainty}\nMatched selection comparators are no intervention, a seeded random coordinate order, and the marginal-aggregate greedy policy. These are policies on the same target, panel, rule, and ceiling, not component ablations. For policy comparisons, define penalized effort $E=|S|/m$ for a verified successful support and $E=1$ otherwise. Assigning one to nonattainment is a reporting convention, not an observed minimum cost. Support plots instead condition on joint attainability and show genuine finite bounds.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L790-L818]]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
