{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"find /usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex -type f -name codex -print; ls /opt/autoillustrate-venv/bin/python; ls /tmp/paper* /workspace/paper/.venv 2>/dev/null; sed -n '80,145p' scripts/curate_references.py; tail -28 scripts/map_claims.py; head -28 references_provenance.json; sed -n '1,70p' paper_strategy.md; cat draft_reference_gaps.md\"","aggregated_output":"/usr/local/lib/node_modules/@openai/codex/node_modules/@openai/codex-linux-x64/vendor/x86_64-unknown-linux-musl/bin/codex\n/opt/autoillustrate-venv/bin/python\n            record[\"bibtex\"] = record[\"bibtex\"].replace(\" and \".join(authors), \" and \".join(authors[:6]) + \" and others\")\n        record[\"bibtex\"] = record[\"bibtex\"].replace(\"&\", r\"\\&\").replace(\"_\", r\"\\_\").replace(\"%\", r\"\\%\")\n        record[\"bibtex\"] = record[\"bibtex\"].replace(\"title={\" + record[\"title\"].replace(\"&\", r\"\\&\") + \"}\", \"title={{\" + record[\"title\"].replace(\"&\", r\"\\&\") + \"}}\")\n    Path(\"references.bib\").write_text(\"\\n\\n\".join(record[\"bibtex\"] for record in records) + \"\\n\")\n    provenance = []\n    for record in records:\n        path = \"literature/\" + record[\"id\"] + \".json\"\n        provenance.append({\"id\": record[\"id\"], \"citation_key\": record[\"id\"], \"title\": record[\"title\"],\n                           \"paper_id\": record[\"url\"], \"query\": \"Exact permitted canonical record: \" + record[\"title\"],\n                           \"source_url\": record[\"url\"], \"retrieved_at\": record[\"retrieved_utc\"],\n                           \"url\": record[\"url\"], \"source\": \"arxiv\" if record[\"id\"] in PAPERS else \"publisher\",\n                           \"evidence_path\": path, \"sha256\": hashlib.sha256(Path(path).read_bytes()).hexdigest(),\n                           \"reading_scope\": record[\"access_scope\"]})\n    Path(\"references_provenance.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"references\": provenance, \"count\": len(provenance),\n        \"generated_at\": datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat(), \"queries\": [item[\"query\"] for item in provenance]}, indent=2))\n    for record in records:\n        print(record[\"id\"], record[\"title\"], \"\\n\", record[\"abstract\"], \"\\n\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n    (\"Every row of the supplementary policy interval table reproduces the recorded family, rule, ceiling, seed, comparator, difference, and interval endpoints.\", EPISODES, [\"derived/paired_intervals.tex\"], []),\n    (\"Auxiliary HELM projections, Open LLM even slots, and BBH odd slots have separately reported counts; BBH revision matching does not establish identical evaluation settings.\", EPISODES, [\"derived/auxiliary.tex\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L718-L732\", \"source_evidence/data/manifest.json#reconciliation\"], []),\n    (\"The completed final evaluation is qualified; this does not imply unrestricted validity or exact large-panel order-statistic costs.\", [\"final.qualification.status\", \"final.qualification.outcome\", \"final.qualification.correct\"], [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation\"], []),\n    (\"The recorded final control pass fraction is one; candidate diagnostics remain unverified.\", [\"final.metric.control_pass_rate\"] + EPISODES, [\"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/control_pass_rate\", \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust\"], []),\n    (\"The retained selection is the reference baseline, with no qualifying development winner and zero retained development-score change.\", [\"study.selection_kind\", \"study.investigation_outcome\", \"study.search_score_delta\"], [\"paper_data.json#/downstream_study\"], []),\n    (\"Three development alternatives were scored and none unscored; their qualification-failure causes are not identified, and they are not controlled component ablations.\", [\"study.attempts.total\", \"study.attempts.scored\", \"study.attempts.unscored\", \"study.investigation_outcome\"], [\"paper_data.json#/downstream_study\"], []),\n    (\"The reported source locations support the exact evaluated implementation, whose selected and baseline source copies match.\", [\"study.selection_kind\"] + EPISODES, [\"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py\", \"source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py\"], []),\n    (\"The final evaluator uses CPU computation, its documented wall and method reservations and advisory decision budget; dependency versions are the supplied versions.\", EPISODES, [\"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L39-L54\", \"source_evidence/evaluation/requirements.txt\", \"derived/environment.json\"], []),\n    (\"Raw-score documentation, normalization, content partitioning, and uncertain score-data redistribution rights are as recorded by the acquisition manifest.\", EPISODES, [\"source_evidence/data/manifest.json\"], []),\n    (\"The study does not measure training responses, practical expenditure, causal contamination effects, or population superiority; the observed ordering is conditional and descriptively selected.\", EPISODES, [\"derived/summary.json#central\", \"source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L16-L28\"], []),\n]\n\n\ndef main():\n    claims = [{\"claim\": claim, \"source_ids\": sources, \"citation_keys\": citations,\n               \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence\"+\"[[\"+path+\"]]\" for path in paths]}\n              for claim, sources, paths, citations in CLAIMS]\n    references = json.loads(Path(\"references_provenance.json\").read_text())[\"references\"]\n    for reference in references:\n        key = reference[\"citation_key\"]\n        claims.append({\"claim\": \"Literature positioning uses only the inspected abstract and canonical metadata of \" + reference[\"title\"] + \".\",\n                       \"source_ids\": [reference[\"id\"]], \"citation_keys\": [key],\n                       \"evidence_refs\": [\"@evidence\"+\"[[literature/\"+key+\".json#abstract]]\"]})\n    Path(\"claims_grounding.json\").write_text(json.dumps({\"claims\": claims}, indent=2)+\"\\n\")\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n{\n  \"campaign_results_dir\": \"work/results/a01/codex/20261117_111437/investigation\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:00:13.897898+00:00\",\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Holistic Evaluation of Language Models\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: The Benchmark Lottery\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Evaluating Agents using Social Choice Theory\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Don't Make Your LLM an Evaluation Benchmark Cheater\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Rethinking Benchmark and Contamination for Language Models with Rephrased Samples\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: GPQA: A Graduate-Level Google-Proof Q&A Benchmark\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Instruction-Following Evaluation for Large Language Models\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: MMLU-Pro: A More Robust and Challenging Multi-Task Language Understanding Benchmark\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Measuring Mathematical Problem Solving With the MATH Dataset\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: MuSR: Testing the Limits of Chain-of-thought with Multistep Soft Reasoning\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Beyond the Imitation Game: Quantifying and extrapolating the capabilities of language models\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Datasheets for Datasets\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Model Cards for Model Reporting\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Beyond Accuracy: Behavioral Testing of NLP models with CheckList\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Underspecification Presents Challenges for Credibility in Modern Machine Learning\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Do ImageNet Classifiers Generalize to ImageNet?\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: TruthfulQA: Measuring How Models Mimic Human Falsehoods\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Array Programming with NumPy\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: SciPy 1.0--Fundamental Algorithms for Scientific Computing in Python\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: Statistical Comparisons of Classifiers over Multiple Data Sets\",\n    \"Exact permitted canonical record: On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation\",\n# Paper strategy: response ceilings and targeted investment\n\n**Status:** ready. **Prompt family:** `paper_standalone` / `write-paper-standalone`.\n**Provisional title:** Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment in Multitask Leaderboards.\n\n## Scientific center\n\n**Question.** Under a bounded, target-only score-response model with fixed rivals, how many task coordinates must be improved to attain strict first place, and how does that investment depend on the aggregation rule and response ceiling?\n\n**Thesis.** In the evaluated public score panels, aggregation robustness is conditional on the response ceiling: exact six-task results reverse the mean–median minimum-support ordering between half and full headroom on matched attainable targets, while larger subject-level panels establish feasible investment bounds rather than exact rule rankings.\n\nThis is a standalone conditional empirical study, not an optimizer breakthrough, a training experiment, or a search report. Organize the six-task and subject-level panels as complementary evidence blocks under one question. Their certificate strength differs; neither is evidence of architecture change or domain transfer. The contribution is the precisely defined investment comparison and its measured limits, not a claim that familiar enumeration or greedy selection is new.\n\n## Framing decision and rejected alternatives\n\nNo edit-context file, previous manuscript, `previous_paper/`, `aggregated/`, or `extra_tasks/` is staged. The JSON status `prior_paper_story_status: rejected` rejects an assumed inherited improvement narrative; there is no actual prior manuscript to reject.\n\n| Candidate framing | Decision and grounded reason |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preserve an optimizer-improvement story | Reject: the frozen selection is the reference baseline, not a successful new optimizer. |\n| Broaden to a shared novel certificate family | Reject: no component ablations substantiate the abandoned proposal mechanisms, and large-panel median/trimmed costs are not exact. |\n| Recenter on response-dependent investment | Choose: final ordinary decision records support direct rule-by-ceiling comparisons, including exact paired contrasts and explicit unattainability. |\n\nDirect inspection confirms that the staged selected and baseline source files are identical. Their small-panel enumeration, exact mean sorting, and larger-panel greedy witnesses determine what can be claimed. The auxiliary projections and BBH records provide additional scope checks, not a reason to broaden the thesis to a new method family. The venue changes presentation, not the scientific conclusion.\n\n## Private grounding and qualification audit\n\nThis section is a provenance memo, not manuscript prose. Use these registered source identifiers in downstream claim mapping:\n\n- `final.qualification.status = completed`, `final.qualification.outcome = qualified`, and `final.qualification.correct = true`. These come from `paper_data.json.final_evaluation`, read independently of selection metadata. Qualification does not establish novelty, population generalization, or every support minimum.\n- `study.selection_kind = baseline`; `study.investigation_outcome = completed_no_winner`; `study.search_score_delta = 0`; `study.attempts.scored = 3`; `study.attempts.unscored = 0`. Search evidence and final measurements have distinct scopes. No search alternative has an established qualifying improvement. Missing performance evidence would not be a measured loss.\n- `final.evidence.episodes` contains three sampling seeds, ordinary and matched-control cases, primary and auxiliary decisions, and policy-comparison intervals. Its `candidate_diagnostics_trust` remains `unverified`. The evidence-file SHA-256 agrees with the manifest. Direct evaluator inspection shows witness verification and exhaustive small-panel reference computation; that does not license unrelated candidate diagnostics. The field `exact_audit_pass` alone is not proof of full-panel exactness.\n- Preserve the final qualification assessment through writing, review, audit, and revision. If authoritative later evidence changes it to a failure, disclose that failure explicitly and retain its limitations. Failed search alternatives have no established causal diagnosis: do not infer performance-only, validity, or adaptivity failure from a boolean outcome.\n\nThe cross-rule cohort check was a read-only summary of existing records, not a new experiment. Restrict to ordinary primary Open LLM decisions and join on seed and target slot. Intersect targets attainable under both mean and median at both half and full headroom. The resulting fixed cohorts contain 18, 17, and 17 targets for seeds 1009, 1013, and 1019. The following rounded values are audit checks derived from `final.evidence.episodes`, not hard-coded plotting inputs:\n\n| Sampling seed | Median minus mean support fraction, half headroom | Same contrast, full headroom |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1009 | -0.129630 | +0.138889 |\n| 1013 | -0.127451 | +0.127451 |\n| 1019 | -0.068627 | +0.156863 |\n\nAll feasible primary six-task costs in this contrast have exact support status and correspond to the frozen exhaustive procedure. The reversal is a descriptive within-snapshot result selected during framing, not a preregistered significance finding. Larger-panel HELM witness-cost reversals are not minimum-cost reversals when certification intervals overlap.\n\n## Scope and method groups\n\nDefine scores in `[0,1]`, a target model, a fixed rival pool, a task panel of size `m`, and selected support `S`. Only selected coordinates change, by `x_j -> x_j + alpha(1-x_j)`, with recorded `alpha` values `0.25`, `0.5`, and `1.0`. Unique first means target aggregate exceeds the largest rival aggregate by more than `1e-10`. Investment is `|S|`, with `|S|/m` used for normalized display. Total normalized score lift is secondary, not dollars, training examples, or compute.\n\nInclude only these method groups:\n\n1. **Frozen support-minimization procedure — supporting.** Exhaust at most six coordinates, sort headroom for larger means, and construct marginal-score-greedy supports for larger median/trimmed panels. Explain the all-coordinate infeasibility test and exact versus lower–upper outcomes.\n2. **Equal-task arithmetic mean — aggregation baseline.** Retain throughout; the headroom solution is exact.\n3. **Conventional median — supporting condition.** Average the two middle scores on even panels; do not substitute a different median convention.\n4. **Symmetric 20-percent trimmed mean — supporting condition.** Remove `floor(m/5)` scores from each tail.\n5. **No-intervention, seeded-random, and marginal-score-greedy policies — selection baselines.** Keep matched recorded comparisons, including ties. They are not component ablations.\n\nDo not require discarded rank-window or clipped-envelope branches in the main narrative. No evaluated Borda condition, equilibrium model, measured training response, architecture intervention, or unrun ablation belongs in this paper. State equal task weights, no spillover, no score regression outside the support, fixed rivals, and uncertain mapping from score uplift to actual training investment.\n\n## Results structure and uncertainty\n\nLead with the exact fixed-cohort mean–median contrast. Follow it with all-target attainability and support/certificate scope for both primary families. Maintain one definition of **target attainment rate**: the fraction of ordinary primary target decisions with a verified strict-first support and a supported claim. Do not mix controls into that rate.\n\nUse a compact main comparator table at half headroom for both families and all three rules, retaining ties with greedy and comparisons against random and no intervention. The complete rule/ceiling/seed grid and all recorded comparator intervals belong after references. A meaningful exact-selection advantage in one small-panel condition does not make the frozen reference a newly invented optimizer.\n\nThe main body must distinguish:\n\n- Exact six-task and mean minimum costs from larger median/trimmed feasible upper bounds and verified lower bounds.\n- Unattainable cases from unresolved support intervals. Never draw the `m+1` infeasibility sentinel as an actual intervention cost.\n- Successful-target support from a penalized effort summary: `final.metric.mean_effort_fraction` assigns one to nonattainment. Likewise, `final.metric.mean_verified_gap_fraction` measures distance to a verified lower bound, not necessarily true optimizer error.\n- Paired comparisons on identical targets from cross-ceiling summaries with different attainable cohorts.\n# Draft Reference Gap Analysis\n\n**Status:** ready\n\nOne medium-priority benchmark-source citation gap remains for the primary Open LLM six-task panel; the existing related-work and results coverage does not justify broader retrieval.\n\n## Review Basis\n\nReviewed `paper.tex`, the 25 entries in `references.bib` and `references_provenance.json`, the 57 claim mappings in `claims_grounding.json`, the grounding-source and final-evaluation records in `paper_data.json`, `paper_strategy.json`, and `global_memory_summary.md`. The memory summary supplies qualitative context only. Existing reference provenance limits substantive reading to canonical metadata and abstracts; this memo does not infer stronger support from those records.\n\n## Target: method-open-llm-panel-source\n\n- **Section:** Method, opening paragraph of “Public score panels and experimental protocol” (`paper.tex:97`).\n- **Priority:** medium.\n- **Citation intent:** benchmark_context.\n- **Anchor:** “the pinned Open LLM contents snapshot with six raw-score coordinates”.\n- **Missing literature:** Official Open LLM leaderboard six-task suite and raw-score documentation.\n- **Reason:** The paragraph names a primary score-panel source without directly citing its official leaderboard or suite documentation. Related Work cites IFEval, BBH, MATH, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro individually, but those sources do not attribute their assembly into this leaderboard panel. No corresponding suite-level source appears among the existing bibliography or provenance entries. The supplementary revision hashes and local acquisition manifest already ground the actual inputs; this is a reader-facing source-attribution gap, not a missing-data or unsupported-results problem.\n- **Focused search topic:** Official Open LLM leaderboard documentation for the six-task suite IFEval, BBH, MATH level 5, GPQA, MuSR, and MMLU-Pro, including raw-score fields.\n- **Minimal later change:** Add one verified canonical official source at this paragraph. If available documentation describes a different leaderboard generation or standardized rather than raw scores, do not treat it as support for the pinned panel. Retain the recorded revisions and normalization details as the authority for the actual analyzed inputs. No method or results rewrite is needed.\n\n## Areas Not Flagged\n\n- Related Work directly cites the benchmark families, social-choice aggregation, robust estimation, leakage and contamination, validity, and uncertainty literature it discusses. No uncited named comparator family there warrants an additional retrieval target.\n- Introduction motivation can use the existing benchmark-composition, social-choice, and contamination references if local citation placement is desired; this does not require new references. The discussion of median and mean behavior is explained by the paper's own definitions rather than asserted as an external finding.\n- Exact enumeration, sorted-headroom optimality, monotonicity, and feasible-support bounds are explained and tied to implementation evidence. The paper disclaims optimizer novelty; adding generic optimization citations would not close a demonstrated gap.\n- Quantitative results, cohort definitions, and certificate scope are grounded in the recorded evaluation and claim mappings, not candidates for bibliographic repair. Real training costs and response calibration remain explicit limitations, not invitations to expand the story.\n\n## Retrieval Boundary\n\nThis memo requests only the single source-attribution lookup above, not a citation-count increase or a broader literature pass. Any later lookup must respect the recorded strategy's prohibited-study and no-citation-graph-expansion restrictions. No reference retrieval was performed, and `paper.tex`, `references.bib`, and `references_provenance.json` were not modified.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
