{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat derived/writer_validation.json; cat derived/page_budget.json; ls /workspace; find /workspace -maxdepth 4 -path '*/src/orchestrator/paper_step.py' -print; sed -n '1,45p' .codex/skills/validate-paper/scripts/validate_figures.py; head -20 paper.tex; cat figures/investment_overview.prompts.json; ls -l figures; cat scientific-evidence/verification-report.json | tail -13\"","aggregated_output":"{\n  \"checked_at\": \"2026-11-17T14:10:33.319304+00:00\",\n  \"checks\": {\n    \"reference_provenance_schema\": true,\n    \"figure_manifest_schema\": true,\n    \"reference_count\": 25,\n    \"grounded_claims\": 57,\n    \"selected_source_identical_to_baseline\": true,\n    \"exact_fixed_cohort_signs\": true,\n    \"independent_final_qualification\": {\n      \"status\": \"completed\",\n      \"outcome\": \"qualified\",\n      \"correct\": true\n    },\n    \"python_syntax\": true,\n    \"visible_language\": true,\n    \"latex_unresolved_or_overfull\": false,\n    \"pdf_pages\": 15,\n    \"pdf_sha256\": \"57ba53930e14f3939c3aae149a3012e4b2a7e868a916b58fbee75ff921a45443\",\n    \"canonical_bibliography_inputs\": true,\n    \"resolved_recorder_inputs\": true,\n    \"approved_system_symlink_normalizations\": 1,\n    \"body_budget\": {\n      \"body_pages\": 8,\n      \"last_page_geometric_fill\": 0.8713280179536451,\n      \"reference_start_page\": 9,\n      \"supplement_start_page\": 11,\n      \"method\": \"Canonical geometric occupancy; VENUE.md bounds applied explicitly.\"\n    },\n    \"dangling_evidence_links\": 0\n  },\n  \"pending\": [\n    \"Orchestrator-owned concept rendering and subsequent full figure validation; placeholder is mandated by the writer-stage contract.\"\n  ]\n}\n{\n  \"body_pages\": 8,\n  \"total_pages\": 15,\n  \"reference_start_page\": 9,\n  \"supplement_start_page\": 11,\n  \"last_body_page_fill_ratio\": 0.8713280179536451,\n  \"min_body_pages\": 8,\n  \"max_body_pages\": 9,\n  \"min_fill_ratio_if_eight_pages\": 0.75,\n  \"passed\": true,\n  \"method\": \"Canonical geometric text/graphics bounds; explicit VENUE.md budget because installed venue catalog is absent.\"\n}downstream\nfinal_evaluation\ninvestigation\nmetrics\npaper\npipeline_events.jsonl\ntask\ntask_drafting\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Validate figure manifest artifacts and basic figure readability budgets.\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nfrom src.orchestrator.paper_step import read_json, validate_figure_manifest\nfrom src.orchestrator.tex_graphics import read_tex_file_bounded\n\n\ndef parse_args() -> argparse.Namespace:\n    \"\"\"Parse command-line arguments.\"\"\"\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=\"Validate figure manifest artifacts.\")\n    parser.add_argument(\"--paper-dir\", default=\".\")\n    return parser.parse_args()\n\n\ndef main() -> int:\n    \"\"\"Run the command-line entry point.\"\"\"\n    args = parse_args()\n    paper_dir = Path(args.paper_dir).resolve()\n    tex_path = paper_dir / \"paper.tex\"\n    paper_data_path = paper_dir / \"paper_data.json\"\n    references_path = paper_dir / \"references_provenance.json\"\n\n    if not tex_path.exists():\n        raise SystemExit(f\"Missing required artifact: {tex_path}\")\n\n    tex_text = read_tex_file_bounded(tex_path)\n    paper_data = read_json(paper_data_path)\n    references = read_json(references_path)\n    grounding_sources = paper_data.get(\"grounding_sources\")\n    if not isinstance(grounding_sources, dict):\n        raise SystemExit(\"paper_data missing grounding_sources object.\")\n    allowed_source_ids = set(str(key) for key in grounding_sources)\n    refs = references.get(\"references\")\n    if isinstance(refs, list):\n        for row in refs:\n            if isinstance(row, dict):\n                source_id = row.get(\"id\")\n                if isinstance(source_id, str) and source_id.strip():\n                    allowed_source_ids.add(source_id)\n\n\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage{iclr2026_conference,times}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,booktabs,graphicx,subcaption,longtable}\n\\usepackage{hyperref,url}\n\\title{Response Ceilings and Targeted Investment\\\\in Multitask Leaderboards}\n\\author{}\n\\newcommand{\\cost}{K}\n\\newcommand{\\eps}{\\varepsilon}\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nMultitask leaderboards compress heterogeneous scores into a single ranking, creating incentives to improve selected tasks. Does replacing the mean with a median make attaining first place more expensive? We study this question under a transparent counterfactual: only a target model changes, each selected score closes a specified fraction of its remaining headroom, and investment counts selected task coordinates. Public HELM and Open LLM score panels permit comparisons of mean, conventional median, and symmetric trimmed mean. On fixed, jointly attainable cohorts in the six-task panel, exact minimum-support comparisons reverse between half and full headroom: median-minus-mean support fractions range from $-0.130$ to $-0.069$ at half headroom and from $+0.127$ to $+0.157$ at full headroom across sampling seeds. Attainability also differs substantially across rules. Larger subject panels yield exact mean costs but only bounded costs for order-statistic rules. Thus aggregation resistance is conditional on response ceilings, target selection, and certificate strength. These are score-space sensitivity results, not measured training costs or evidence of real contamination effects.\n% @evidence[[derived/summary.json#central]]\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\nMultitask benchmarks make model comparison manageable by replacing a vector of task scores with an overall ranking. Their breadth is valuable: knowledge, instruction following, and reasoning need not improve together. But this compression also defines incentives. A developer interested in one model's position may concentrate effort on selected benchmark components rather than improving uniformly. The resulting question is not merely whether rankings change when a task is added or removed. It is how much targeted change to an existing score vector is sufficient to move a fixed target above every rival.\n\nIt is tempting to regard an order-statistic aggregator as intrinsically more resistant to concentrated investment than an arithmetic mean. The mean responds to every coordinate, whereas the median depends on central values and a trimmed mean discards tails. This intuition does not by itself answer the ranking question. Raising a weak score can have substantial effect on the mean but none on the median until the score crosses a central rank. Conversely, a median threshold may be reachable by modifying a relatively small set of central coordinates, while a mean threshold still requires improvement spread across many tasks. The permitted magnitude of each change is therefore part of the question, not an incidental nuisance parameter.\n{}\ntotal 248\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  17656 Nov 17 14:03 attainability_and_support_scope.pdf\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    485 Nov 17 14:03 attainability_and_support_scope.pdf.prov.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent 128396 Nov 17 14:03 attainability_and_support_scope.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    485 Nov 17 14:03 attainability_and_support_scope.png.prov.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  11916 Nov 17 14:03 exact_rule_contrast.pdf\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    473 Nov 17 14:03 exact_rule_contrast.pdf.prov.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  57333 Nov 17 14:03 exact_rule_contrast.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    473 Nov 17 14:03 exact_rule_contrast.png.prov.json\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   9302 Nov 17 13:48 investment_overview.png\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent      3 Nov 17 13:48 investment_overview.prompts.json\n    }\n  ],\n  \"root_dir\": \"/workspace/paper\",\n  \"schema_name\": \"scientific-evidence.verification-report\",\n  \"